\par\par \b \uc2 ECB Weidmann: Condemns Political  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  in Monetary Policy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Todd Buell   \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2013\par 19:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Governments across the world are intruding in the realm of monetary policy, risking the inflation-fighting achievements of past years and endangering economic stability, \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member \uc2 Jens Weidmann\uc2  said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Weidmann, who also serves as the president of the \uc2 German \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bundesbank, took aim specifically at the governments of Japan and Hungary, who in his words had "encroached" on the activity of their \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Weidmann further warned against countries turning to the exchange rate as a political tool. He said a consequence of politicians meddling with central bankers, "whether intended or not, could lead to an increasingly politicized exchange rate. Until now, the international monetary system has come through the crisis without a race to devaluation, and I really hope that it stays that way."\par \par \uc2 "Questionable encroachments are already visible, for example in Hungary or \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where the governments are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the duties of the central banks, pressuring for a more aggressive monetary policy that threatens the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  autonomy," said Mr. Weidmann according to the text of a speech to be delivered Monday evening at the headquarters of \uc2 Deutsche Boerse AG\uc2  (DB1.XE) and provided by the Bundesbank in advance.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Weidmann's remarks are also significant as it is relatively unusual for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official to take issue publicly with the policies of other countries. The head of the Bundesbank also took a small swipe at activities on the other side of the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 He said the recently agreed budget compromise in the United States to avoid the "fiscal cliff" is "not a sustainable solution to the fiscal problems." He added that raising the debt ceiling or delayed expenditure cuts "could indeed quickly lead to the next showdown. What is necessary here is more determined action in the common interest."\par \par \uc2 The Bundesbank--founded from the ashes of the Second World War--is widely viewed as an anchor of stability in Germany, an institution that citizens believe will maintain the value of hard-earned savings.\par \par \uc2 European policy makers have also increased pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take on new tasks. For example, they are aiming to give the ECB some banking supervisory powers as part of a "banking union" on the continent. German observers are, however, worried that this will blur the banks' primary goal of keeping inflation just below 2% over the medium term. In his speech, Mr. Weidmann stressed that the creation of a banking union "shouldn't create new conflicts of interest that distract monetary policy from its primary goal of monetary stability."\par \par \uc2 There is also concern in Germany that European politicians have relied too heavily on central banks to solve the financial crisis at the expense of necessary fiscal reform. Mr. Weidmann said in a recent interview that monetary policy is like a drug in the sense that it "attacks the symptoms but does not solve the origins and also comes with risks and side effects."\par \par \uc2 Both Japan and Hungary have come under international scrutiny for alleged political meddling in monetary policy. In Japan, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come under pressure from newly-elected Prime Minister Shinzo Abe to ramp up money printing.\par \par \uc2 In Hungary, markets are worried that a new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, due to replace outgoing head Andras Simor, will follow the wishes of Prime Minister Viktor Orban and print money as a way to spark growth but the consequence of which is to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Brian Blackstone and Laura Stevens contributed to this report.)\par \par \uc2 Write to Todd Buell at todd.buell@dowjones.com \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @ToddBuell [ 21-01-13 1800GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130121e91l000ob\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
