\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-Japan finmin: govt wants to firm up its position on BOJ inflation target in Jan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto   \par \uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2012\par 12:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Corrects to clarify that Aso said the government wants to firm up its position on an inflation target accord, not reach on agreement with the BOJ on the target, before the BOJ meeting)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 28 (Reuters) - New Japanese Finance Minister Taro Aso said on Friday that he wants the government to firm up its position on an accord with the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  over an increased inflation target before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next policy meeting in January, to attack the country's entrenched deflation.\par \par \uc2 Aso, speaking to a small group of reporters, also said Japanese authorities stood ready to act against speculators driving the yen up or down excessively, saying that such moves could cause difficulties for the world's third-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Shinzo Abe's government is pursuing a policy mix of aggressive monetary easing and heavy fiscal spending to beat deflation and weaken the yen, calling on the BOJ to adopt a 2 percent inflation target - double its current target.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the timing of a policy accord on the inflation target, Aso, a former prime minister, said it could come in January after the government maps out an extra stimulus budget and before the BOJ holds its next policy meeting on Jan. 21-22.\par \par \uc2 "It'll be clear how the government is responding (on the economy) when we compile an extra budget in January ... We must then consider (an accord) before the BOJ's policy meeting," Aso told reporters in a group interview.\par \par \uc2 The government will compile spending requests for a stimulus package on Jan. 7 and finalise the proposal shortly thereafter.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ eased policy last week, and has promised to debate setting a new price target at the Jan. 21-22 meeting.\par \par \uc2 The yen fell to its lowest level in more than two years versus the dollar on Friday, pressured by expectations that the new Japanese government will push the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into more aggressive monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 "If excessive rises or falls in the yen due to speculation cause trouble for a lot of people, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a powerful tool, so there's no reason why we would not use it," Aso said.\par \par \uc2 "Under the current situation where movements are gradual, I think it should basically be left to market mechanisms and fundamentals," he added.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether Japan's efforts to ease monetary policy and weaken the yen may lead to competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations, Aso said: "It's wrong to say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  unreasonably."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ASO (URGENT, PIX) (CORRECTED) | ABN | D | E | RBN | J | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | RNP | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121228e8cs000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
