\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Japan's Call for Weaker Yen Spurs Talk of Copycat Moves\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi And Tatsuo Ito   \par \uc2 1,167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2012\par 01:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Unusual explicitness from Japan's new leaders has helped convince the market they are serious about weakening the yen to revive the nation's embattled exporters, but such moves threaten to complicate Tokyo's relations with the U.S. and other major trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Many central banks, including others in Asia as well as in Latin America, have been trying to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from becoming too strong after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopted super-loose monetary policies that have suppressed their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Confronted by weakening domestic growth, nations around the world are trying to support their economies by boosting exports. Weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  make a country's exports cheaper. Japan's latest signals mark a bid to help its exporters as the yen faces upward pressure from investors fleeing other trouble spots.\par \par \uc2 Senior Japanese officials, led by Prime Minister Shinzo Abe, have signaled they are determined to keep the dollar above 85 yen through further monetary easing and other bold measures.\par \par \uc2 The dollar broke above Y86 after Asian markets closed Thursday, hitting a 28-month high of Y86.16. The yen has since regained some ground, capitalizing on its status as a haven, after Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Harry Reid\uc2  (D., Nev.) warned the U.S. may go over the fiscal cliff, triggering automatic spending cuts and tax increases economists say could push the U.S. into recession. At Thursday's peak, the dollar had climbed about 11% this quarter.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Abe made more-aggressive policy one of the centerpieces of his winning election campaign, and analysts and investors say he has been so successful that there could be copycats among other nations looking to see a similar result.\par \par \uc2 "This is another salvo in what you might call the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," said Richard Franulovich, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Westpac Banking\uc2  in New York. "Abe has taken a radical approach and people are learning from that lesson."\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, which is wrestling with a surge in the won this year, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was particularly blunt Thursday, saying it said it would prioritize economic recovery when it reviews its official interest rate each month.\par \par \uc2 Other countries in recent years--including Israel and Switzerland--have taken strong action to prevent their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too much and hurting exporters. Brazil stoked international tensions two years ago by complaining of a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," blaming the U.S., Europe and China and others for pushing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower to boost their competitiveness. Leaders in those regions have said they are only trying to support their own economies.\par \par \uc2 Brazil has said the flood of cheap money flowed into the country searching for higher returns from higher interest rates. The effect was to inflate the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which in turn made it harder for local manufacturers to compete. Despite chiding other nations for weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Brazilian officials have sought to weaken the real, by putting restrictions on foreign investment and reducing interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Australia has also been wrestling with the impact of a high \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Australian dollar breached US$1.10 in July 2011--the first time since it was floated in 1983--and has remained at elevated levels for much of the following 18 months despite a weakening in commodity prices. This sharp run higher has hurt the nation's manufacturing, education and tourism industries--three of the largest sectors of the country's economy.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, however, the sharp moves in the dollar-yen exchange rate have drawn cheers from domestic manufacturers, whose products become more competitive in the international market when the yen falls relative to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A weak yen is "good news" for Japanese firms and could have "a significant impact" on their bottom lines, said \uc2 Tadashi Okamura\uc2 , head of the Japan Chamber of Commerce and Industry, a major business organization. "That's because companies have compiled business plans forecasting below-Y80 levels."\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, Mr. Abe said that with the dollar above Y85, "companies that haven't been paying taxes until now can pay taxes" because a weaker yen lifts profit. Those remarks came on the top of comments Friday by Shigeru Ishiba, the ruling Liberal Democratic Party's No. 2: "We need to think about how to maintain [the dollar] around Y85-Y90."\par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Akira Amari added his voice on Thursday, saying the yen "is making headway toward the appropriate level" and that the government "must ensure that this movement will take hold."\par \par \uc2 A weaker yen could inflate Japan's import bill by making overseas goods more expensive. Still, the overall economic impact of yen drops would be positive until the dollar rises sharply above Y100, said Toshihiro Nagahama, chief economist at Dai-ichi Life Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Abe's quest to undercut the yen has won sympathy from some government policy makers irked by years of yen strength. Yet it has raised concerns abroad that he may stir tensions in the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "There's a risk Japan would be misunderstood by foreign countries" as trying to manipulate the yen's levels to the advantage of its companies, a Japanese government official said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market invited a harsh reprimand from the U.S. Treasury Department in late 2011, despite Tokyo's argument that the actions were legitimate. U.S. officials said at the time that Japan should take action to address more fundamental problems in its economy rather than resort to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Both countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can face upward pressure from global haven flows, particularly during international crises.\par \par \uc2 Former Finance Minister Jun Azumi, who oversaw Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late 2011, has privately told subordinates that U.S. officials complained to him "on various occasions" about the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a former aide.\par \par \uc2 Should the new finance minister, Taro Aso, also go out of his way to cite lower yen levels as favorable, "that could invite a considerable amount of booing and complaint from the U.S. and Europe," said Kenji Yumoto, vice chairman of the Japan Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 Mentioning preferred exchange-rate levels also tends to trigger speculation about when Japan's authorities will step into the market to artificially influence the yen's levels. Such speculation often stokes volatility.\par \par \uc2 "People would start asking, 'Will the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar tops Y90? Will it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the dollar slips below Y85?,'" the government official said.\par \par \uc2 But having been well-briefed by finance ministry officials on the pros and cons of commenting on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, Mr. Aso is unlikely to cross the line, government officials said. Other cabinet ministers will probably stop discussing yen levels once they settle into their roles, they said.\par \par \uc2 --Alexander Martin, Geoffrey Rogow, John Lyons and Sudeep Reddy contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com and Tatsuo Ito at tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com [ 12-27-12 1926ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121228e8cs0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
