\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(12/24) Global  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Tensions Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2012\par 03:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL)\par \par \uc2 By Tatsuo Ito and William Mallard\par \par \uc2 TOKYO -- Japan's incoming prime minister fired a volley into increasingly tense global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, saying the country must defend itself against attempts by other governments to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by ensuring the yen weakens as well.\par \par \uc2 Shinzo Abe's call comes as others including Bank of England Gov. \uc2 Mervyn King\uc2  warn that the world's economic-policy makers risk becoming embroiled in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spats that could heighten tensions among countries.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Abe on Sunday called on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to resist what he described as moves by the U.S. and Europe to cheapen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and noted that a yen level of around 90 yen to the dollar -- it was at <yen>84.38 in early Asian trading Monday, down from 84.26 yen late Friday -- would support the profit of Japanese exporters.\par \par \uc2 "Central banks around the world are printing money, supporting their economies and increasing exports. America is the prime example," said Mr. Abe, referring to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's policy of flooding the market with dollars by purchasing massive amounts of Treasury bonds and other assets.\par \par \uc2 "If it goes on like this, the yen will inevitably strengthen. It's vital to resist this," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. King, in an interview this month, said, "I do think 2013 could be a challenging year in which we will, in fact, see a number of countries trying to push down their exchange rates. That does lead to concerns."\par \par \uc2 It was part of an effort by countries to preserve trade advantage, he said. "The policies pursued by countries for domestic purposes are leading to tension collectively."\par \par \uc2 What is notable about Messrs. Abe's and King's comments is that the scope of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  angst seems to be expanding. China, which manages its exchange rate to keep it closely aligned with the U.S. dollar, has long been the object of global criticism for its efforts to hold down the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to boost exports.\par \par \uc2 Since the financial crisis, other countries -- including Switzerland, Israel and South Korea -- have ramped up their efforts to prevent their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from getting too strong amid worries about their export competitiveness. Policy makers in Australia also are under increasing pressure to fight the rise of the Australian dollar.\par \par \uc2 Global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves expanded to $10.5 trillion by mid-2012 from $6.7 trillion in 2007, according to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , a 57% rise in less than five years and a sign of how aggressively world central banks are stockpiling other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to prevent their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from getting too strong in the wake of the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The largest increase has been in Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 It is "completely different" for Japanese companies if the dollar is in the 80-yen range, as it is now, as opposed to the 90s yen, Mr. Abe said. If the dollar "is above 85 yen, companies that haven't been paying taxes until now [because they don't have profit]. . .can pay taxes."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. hasn't explicitly sought a weaker dollar. But the effect of its policies has been to suppress its value. Most notably, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's quantitative-easing programs -- in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prints dollars to purchase government bonds -- have the side effect of holding down the international value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by increasing its supply in global markets.\par \par \uc2 Despite this effect, the dollar has retained much of its global trading value in recent years because investors are flocking into \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds as safe haven investments.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Index, a broad trade-weighted measure of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's global strength, has fallen from a high of 78.68, during the worst of the global financial crisis in November 2008, to 70.17 in Asia trade Monday, as the Fed has undertaken several rounds of "quantitative easing" and as the return of some calm to financial markets has eased safe-haven buying pressure on the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Some prominent U.S. economists have been pressing the Fed and \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  to respond more aggressively to Chinese actions. Fred Bergsten and Joseph Gagnon, economists at the Peterson Institute for International Economics, estimate that the U.S. trade deficit would be $150 billion to $300 billion smaller -- and the U.S. would have two million more jobs -- if China and other emerging markets didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They have called on U.S. policy makers to retaliate, by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to hold down the dollar or by taxing imports from these countries.\par \par \uc2 Low-interest-rate policies and quantitative-easing strategies like the Fed's are one way to suppress the value of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Another is \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- in which a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buys another. \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November sold won and bought at least $1 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb a steep rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said, and its officials warned against "excessive" moves that would hurt the nation's exporters.\par \par \uc2 Such \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  historically have limited impacts. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reined in its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, following an unusual criticism of its forays into the market by the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Abe himself takes a dim view of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying in a November interview with The Journal that it is "hardly effective."\par \par \uc2 Instead, he is ramping up pressure on the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  for aggressive steps including "unlimited easing" to whip the country's chronic deflation and keep the yen's strength in check.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, Mr. Abe repeated calls for the bank to set a firm 2% target for price inflation at its January policy-board meeting, and threatened to take legislative action to force the bank's hand if it doesn't act on its own.\par \par \uc2 Edwin Truman\uc2 , another economist at the Peterson Institute, warns that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  retaliations could become destabilizing if taken too far.\par \par \uc2 "If you allow a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be dramatically undervalued, then you are also are going to invite trade wars," Mr. Truman said in an interview last week.\par \par \uc2 Trade wars, in which countries restrict imports from other countries, were an important feature of Depression-era policies in the 1930s which crimped global economic growth. Mr. Truman said he had grown concerned that cooperation between countries on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions had diminished in recent years.\par \par \uc2 If it continues, he said, then "you go from a world in which there is a broad level of cooperation on monetary measures to one in which it is every man for himself," he said.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ last week said it would consider a price target at its January meeting, and unveiled what it called an unprecedented program to provide cheap funds to commercial banks in return for an increase in their loans -- including loans to finance acquisitions overseas.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's increasing willingness to say that its policies can have the effect of weakening the yen.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary easing in itself won't be a problem. But if that is linked to a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that will be viewed as a beggar-thy-neighbor policy," said Osamu Takashima, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Abe and other heavyweights in his Liberal Democratic Party appear to be favoring only a moderate weakening of the yen, since the country's import tab has shot up following the nuclear accident of March 2011.\par \par \uc2 The accident effectively resulted in the idling of most of Japan's nuclear plants, and fossil-fuel purchases have surged as a result.\par \par \uc2 "Given Japan's industrial structure, it's not the case of the weaker the yen the better," said Shigeru Ishiba, the LDP's No. 2 ranked politician and a key Abe lieutenant, in a TV interview Friday, according to local media reports. "We need to think about how to maintain it around 85 yen-90 yen" to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Yuji Saito, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  in Tokyo, said Mr. Abe's team is sending "a wise message that what they want is just to correct the extremely strong yen -- not to pursue weakening the yen. The 85 yen-90 yen range should be a comfortable zone for everyone including exporters, importers and banks as well as international partners such as South Korea and the U.S."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Takashi Mochizuki and In-Soo Nam contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 24-12-12 0235GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020121224e8co00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
