\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/MarketBeat: A Weaker Yen? \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Says Don't Bet On It\b0\par\par\uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2012\par 19:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Market Beat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Stephen Grocer\par \par \uc2 Traders may be getting ahead of themselves in betting on a weaker yen.\par \par \uc2 Expectations are high that Japanese opposition leader Shinzo Abe will become the next prime minister following elections scheduled for Dec. 16. Given his vocal support for more radical efforts at the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to weaken the yen, investors see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the best way to bet on political change.\par \par \uc2 But, as \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy team argues in a research note today, doubts are emerging about whether Abe will be able to deliver on his election promises, and given that investors are now positioned for yen weakness to continue, "the pullback could be acute."\par \par \uc2 In the options market, the so-called risk-reversal skew - which can serve as a gauge of which direction investors expect a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move - shows the strongest preference for bets that the yen will weaken against the dollar since 2001. And in the spot market, the yen is down almost 5% since late September, with the dollar/yen rate trading recently at Y81.82.\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2  points out that several of Abe's proposals are fairly radical, and may eventually be delayed or watered down. The suggestion that the BOJ could buy government-issued construction bonds may be illegal, since it would amount to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly underwriting the government. Purchases of foreign bonds might be viewed as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is the sacred purview of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not the BOJ's.\par \par \uc2 Finally, raising the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation target, which would theoretically result in an unprecedented round of money printing, might not be as effective as investors expect. Since 2010, the BOJ has held rates near zero and started purchasing bonds, but that hasn't kept the yen from strengthening.\par \par \uc2 "Should we really sell the JPY now on the basis of policy changes which may or may not change sometime in the middle of 2013?" \uc2 HSBC\uc2  asks.\par \par \uc2 The U.K.-based bank concludes that, like the many cycles of yen weakness seen over the past decade, this one will eventually reverse. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  is maintaining its forecast that the yen will strengthen by the end of the year, sending the dollar/yen rate back down to Y78.\par \par \uc2 Normal\par \par \uc2 0\par \par \uc2 false\par \par \uc2 false\par \par \uc2 false\par \par \uc2 EN-US\par \par \uc2 X-NONE\par \par \uc2 X-NONE\par \par \uc2 MicrosoftInternetExplorer4\par \par \uc2 In the options market, the so-called risk-reversal skew - which can serve as a gauge of which direction investors expect a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move - shows the strongest preference for bets that the yen will weaken against the dollar since 2001.\par \par \uc2 table.MsoNormalTable\par \par \uc2 \{mso-style-name:"Table Normal";\par \par \uc2 mso-tstyle-rowband-size:0;\par \par \uc2 mso-tstyle-colband-size:0;\par \par \uc2 mso-style-noshow:yes;\par \par \uc2 mso-style-priority:99;\par \par \uc2 mso-style-qformat:yes;\par \par \uc2 mso-style-parent:"";\par \par \uc2 mso-padding-alt:0in 5.4pt 0in 5.4pt;\par \pa