\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ INTERVIEW:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  LDP's Abe Says  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Ineffective\b0\par\par\uc2 1,027 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2012\par 22:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  LDP's Abe says not thinking of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push yen lower\par \par \uc2 --Mr. Abe says wants BOJ to address strong yen through monetary policy\par \par \uc2 --No need to revise BOJ law if it adopts 2% inflation "target," he says\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By George Nishiyama, Yuka Hayashi and Alexander Martin \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO--Liberal Democratic Party leader Shinzo Abe, whose intense calls for drastic monetary easing have talked down the yen, said that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to drive the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower are not on his mind and have been ineffective.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Abe is seen as the front-runner to be the country's next prime minister ahead of a Dec. 16 general election and financial markets have been following his comments closely.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent days, the yen has weakened to a seven-month low against the dollar, pushing up domestic share prices, largely on expectations that if Mr. Abe were to take the helm, that would lead to more aggressive easing steps by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 . His party has set beating deflation and the strong yen as a top priority in its campaign platform.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Mr. Abe said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market was not the way to go, especially if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had to act on its own.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a measure that's not at all in my mind now," Mr. Abe told the Wall Street Journal. "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are hardly effective. They haven't been effective so far. It's difficult to get international coordination."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked whether he was satisfied with seeing the yen being pushed lower by his comments, he said he would not touch on specific \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's not about whether the yen is stronger or weaker. It has to be at an appropriate level."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, he turned to the BOJ to address the yen's strength through monetary policy, saying that increasing money in the economy would "correct" the strong yen as well as bring about an end to deflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We should not manipulate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates. The strong yen will be corrected if we take policies to get out of deflation," said Mr. Abe, who was prime minister for a year from September 2006, adding that the BOJ should work to increase the monetary base.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under the current government of Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda, Japanese authorities have stepped into the market to sell the yen to rein in its strength, but they have stayed away for a year after its solo yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last November was met by criticism from Washington.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen hasn't since departed significantly from its historical high of Y75.31 against the dollar. The dollar rose to Y82.59 on EBS on Thursday, its highest level since April 4.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Abe again called on the BOJ to make a stronger commitment to beat deflation, saying that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needed to set a clear inflation target, as opposed to the weaker "goal" that it now seeks. He added that a rare joint statement issued by the BOJ and the government last month pledging a common front against deflation wasn't enough.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It didn't include an inflation target. That's what's wrong about it, that's what's different from our policy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 58-year-old lawmaker said the BOJ needed to state clearly that it will seek a 2% inflation target, as opposed to aiming for the near-term "goal" of 1% inflation that it does now. He added that if he became the nation's leader again, he would appoint a BOJ governor who would commit to the 2% inflation target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "When one says 'the skies will be clear the day after tomorrow,' that doesn't imply there's a responsibility to make it clear," he said, criticizing BOJ's current policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he softened his stance on whether BOJ governor should be fired if the target is not met, or if a law revision was necessary to strip away the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's independence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If it agrees with our 2% target and commits to it, then there is no need to revise the BOJ law," Mr. Abe said, adding that if the bank fails to achieve the price target, its governor simply needed to be held responsible in explaining why.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I feel that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's independence is vital. The problem is that (in Japan) it's out of line with the global standard. Its policy target should be shared with the government, but it's important that the BOJ retain its independence over implementing policy measures," Mr. Abe said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The problem in Japan is that the BOJ has independence over both the measures and the target."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked about concerns that an emphasis on ending deflation may lead to a lack of fiscal discipline for the country whose outstanding debt is already over twice the size of its economy, the former prime minister said any rebuilding of the state finances can only be achieved after overcoming deflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "First, we will concentrate our policy tools to beat deflation. But that can't be done solely by monetary policy. Fiscal policy needs to be added." Mr. Abe's LDP has called for a large extra spending package to give a boost to the economy. It also has proposed a Y200 trillion package of public works projects to "strengthen the nation's infrastructure."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Abe also said the government should not first put a numerical limit on the issuance of new debt, as past governments have done. Mr. Noda's government has kept issuance below Y44 trillion, the amount issued under the last LDP government in 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It should not start with a number," he said, adding that the government should try to curb debt issuance looking at the economic condition at the time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Unless you get out of deflation, you won't have nominal growth, and there won't be any fiscal reconstruction."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to George Nishiyama at george.nishiyama@dowjones.com, Yuka Hayashi at yuka.hayashi@wsj.com and Alexander Martin at alexander.martin@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribe to WSJ: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2 \f2  [ 22-11-12 2130GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121122e8bm000sd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
