\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-UPDATE 1-Japan govt open to compromise to avoid "fiscal cliff"\b0\par\par\uc2 909 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2012\par 08:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Updates with more comments)\par \par \uc2  * Deficit financing bill absolute priority, deal with opposition eyed\par \par \uc2  * Opposition must present its conditions for compromise-vice finmin\par \par \uc2  * U.S., Japan fiscal problems, euro crisis main economic risks \par \par \uc2  By Tetsushi Kajimoto and Shinji Kitamura\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 1 (Reuters) - Japan's government is ready to compromise with the opposition to pass a crucial bill needed to prevent a crippling funding shortfall, Vice Finance Minister Tsutomu Okubo said on Thursday, urging the opposition to spell out terms.\par \par \uc2  The main opposition Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) has been  pressing Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda to call an early election, using the funding issue to beggar the government into submission at a time when the world's third largest economy stands at risk of sliding into recession.\par \par \uc2   "If we were to choose one bill that we wanted to enact in the current session, it would be the deficit bond bill," Okubo told Reuters in an interview. "The situation could turn into a Japanese fiscal cliff."\par \par \uc2  That term was coined to describe massive tax hikes and cuts to public spending that could hit the United States at the start of 2013 if the Congress fails to act.\par \par \uc2   To prevent something similar happening in Japan, Noda called an extra parliament session running until Nov. 30 to pass the bill to issue deficit-financing bonds, but needs backing from the opposition, which controls the upper house and can block legislation there.\par \par \uc2   Otherwise, Japan could run out of money by the end of this month and would have to stop debt auctions in December. The government is already delaying some spending on tax grants to rural governments, some government affiliate agencies and univesities to avoid running out of cash.\par \par \uc2  Without more funds, the government would have to dip into reserves to service debt and avoid a default, and could be forced to cut benefits to unemployed, salaries for civil servants, and transfers to rural goverments. \par \par \uc2   Credit ratings agencies have warned over the dangers posed by political deadlock. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  on Thursday reiterated its concern with the prolonged standoff that left the government unable to borrow 38.3 trillion yen ($479.17 billion) needed to cover the budget deficit and finance about 40 percent of its spending for the fiscal year ending in March 2013.\par \par \uc2  Okubo said passage of the bill was the utmost priority for the government, aware that a failure to do so would have grave consequences for markets and the economy.\par \par \uc2  "I want ruling and opposition parties to sit at a negotiating table and come up with concrete proposals. We should then quickly respond after looking at those proposals," Okubo said.\par \par \uc2  The opposition had forced Noda in August to promise an election "soon". But with his Democratic Party of Japan (DPJ) scoring poorly in opinion polls, Noda has been coy on the exact timing of an election that must be held by August 2013.\par \par \uc2  Some opposition lawmakers have suggested a compromise was possible if the government met other demands, such as cutting some spending in the current budget.\par \par \uc2  Okubo said it was premature to make any response.\par \par \uc2  "I'm aware some people say we should tweak the budget to cut spending, but the Liberal Democratic Party as a whole has not made such a proposal yet. We cannot respond until we look at a concrete proposal."\par \par \uc2  STABILITY\par \par \uc2  Speaking ahead of a \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  finance chiefs' Nov. 4-5 meeting in Mexico, Okubo also said fiscal problems of Japan and the United States along with Europe's sovereign debt crisis and the slowdown in emerging market economies all posed major risks to global economy.\par \par \uc2  While Tokyo has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for nearly a year, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long argued that one of the unwanted side effects of the euro crisis was capital flight to the relative safety of the yen, driving it beyond levels that its exporters can cope with.\par \par \uc2  "If there's a discussion (at G20), we want to maintain the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability as excess volatility and disorderly movements hurt the economy," Okubo told a news conference later on Thursday, after speaking to Reuters.\par \par \uc2  The G20 meeting was also likely to discuss the impact of U.S. financial regulation, including a so-called Volcker rule setting limits on banks' own-account trading, Okubo told Reuters.\par \par \uc2  Turning to the Bank of Japan's monetary easing earlier this week and an unprecedented joint statement with the government pledging continued efforts to battle deflation, Okubo said the move demonstrated resolve to beat deflation and deal with a strong yen.\par \par \uc2  Due to a decline in long-term yields, Obuko said there was no pressing need for the BOJ to consider extending maturities of bonds bought under its asset purchase programme, something he had urged earlier. But, he added that it could remain a an option in the longer-run.\par \par \uc2  He did, however, recommend that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought  bonds of utilities as part of its corporate debt purchases.\par \par \uc2  "I personally hope that the BOJ will steadily buy bonds issued by electricity companies, which are the weakest but most influential in the corporate bond sector," Okubo said.     \par \par \uc2 ($1 = 79.9300 Japanese yen)  \par \par \uc2 (Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/OKUBO (UPDATE 1, INTERVIEW, PIX) | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | J | ABX | BNX | FUN | G | RBN | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS | SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121101e8b1000g4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
