\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Japan MOF Nakao: Joint Govt/BOJ Statement "Most Important" Outcome From BOJ Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 875 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2012\par 12:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 30 Oct 2012 07:10 EDT *DJ  Japan Nakao: Joint Statement Shows "Decisiveness, Resolution" Of BOJ, Govt To Beat Deflation\par \par \uc2 30 Oct 2012 07:10 EDT *DJ  Japan Nakao: Hope Mkt Will Recognize Importance of BOJ-Govt Decision To Issue Statement\par \par \uc2 30 Oct 2012 07:10 EDT *DJ  Japan Nakao: Monetary Policy Not "Single Effective Policy" To Stabilize Yen\par \par \uc2 30 Oct 2012 07:10 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Nakao: Still Believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Needed When Appropriate\par \par \uc2 30 Oct 2012 07:10 EDT *DJ  Japan Nakao: Recent Mitigation Of Upward Pressure On Yen Welcome\par \par \uc2 30 Oct 2012 07:10 EDT *DJ  Japan Nakao: Preparing Technical Elements Of Planned Investment In China Govt Debt\par \par \uc2 30 Oct 2012 07:10 EDT *DJ  Japan Nakao: Don't Know When Japan Will Buy China Govt Debt\par \par \uc2 30 Oct 2012 07:10 EDT *DJ  Japan Nakao: G-20 Fin Min Talks To Focus On Global Econ, Euro Crisis, Fiscal Consolidation\par \par \uc2 30 Oct 2012 07:15 EDT DJ  INTERVIEW: Joint Govt/BOJ Statement "Most Important" Outcome Of BOJ Meeting\par \par \uc2 --Japan's top financial diplomat says the BOJ's most important action at its policy board meeting was a joint statement with the government renewing the commitment to beating deflation\par \par \uc2 --The official's remarks could add to speculation the government has strengthened its grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy and may more aggressively call for further monetary easing\par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  chief Japan economist says the statement could be a first step toward a government-BOJ policy accord \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO--Japan's top financial diplomat said Tuesday that the "most important" action by the Bank of Japan earlier in the day wasn't its decision to further ease monetary policy, but a joint statement with the government in which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  renewed its commitment to beating deflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The remarks by Vice Finance Minister Takehiko Nakao could add to speculation that the government has strengthened its grip on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, and may use the statement to more aggressively call for further monetary easing should Japan's economic downturn worsen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The most important element is that they agreed to issue a joint statement, a joint announcement to make efforts together, and this shows the decisiveness, resolution of ... the BOJ and the government" to reversing years of growth-stunting price declines, Mr. Nakao said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Of course the independence of the BOJ is important, but joint efforts ... by the BOJ and the government are really needed, and it is clearly stated in the joint announcement," Mr. Nakao said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under pressure from the government to do more to revitalize the economy, the BOJ's policy board voted unanimously earlier in the day to increase the size of its asset purchases to Y91 trillion from Y80 trillion previously, and to introduce a new lending facility to encourage loans by banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The steps came with a rare statement, signed by BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa, Finance Minister Koriki Jojima and Economy Minister Seiji Maehara, pledging the government and the BOJ will "work together and make their utmost efforts" to address deflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The content of the statement itself isn't especially new, and the market zeroed in on the monetary policy action and pushed the yen higher versus the dollar, a move that threatens Japan's export-reliant economy. "I hope the market will recognize the importance of this decision" to release the statement, Mr. Nakao said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Naohiko Baba, chief Japan economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 , noted in a report the potential significance of the statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We see its key implication as a formal tightening of the government's grip on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Mr. Baba said, adding that it could be "a first step toward a government-BOJ policy accord."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a fresh hint at potential government action to weaken the yen, Mr. Nakao--Japan's most influential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy bureaucrat--said direct finance ministry \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market still has a role in stabilizing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think monetary policy is ... the single effective policy to have a stable exchange rate," Mr. Nakao said. "I still believe that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is needed when it is appropriate."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy to "act decisively when the market moves in very excessive or disorderly ways has not changed at all."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citing some weakening in the yen against the dollar in recent weeks, Mr. Nakao said "some mitigation, alleviation of pressure in the exchange market in the recent weeks is welcome." But "I wouldn't say if the present level is ... comfortable to us."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar stood at Y79.40 around 1000 GMT, slightly above the average forecast rate of big Japanese manufacturers for the current fiscal year ending in March, but still below where it was before the BOJ's latest announcement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Nakao said a meeting of finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chiefs from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of 20\f28 \uc2 \f2  leading industrial and developing economies in Mexico City next week will likely discuss the global economy, the euro-zone debt crisis and fiscal overhaul.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 30, 2012 07:15 ET (11:15 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121030e8au003ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
