\par\par \b \uc2 DJ BOJ WATCH: BOJ Could Face More Pressure To Ease Amid Lack of G-7 Support For  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2012\par 10:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Last week's annual meetings of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and related events demonstrated the difficulty of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  winning foreign backing for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say\par \par \uc2 --The IMF may not be as supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as some had thought\par \par \uc2 --The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may face more pressure in the coming days to ease policy further \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO--A series of high-profile international financial meetings last week demonstrated how hard it is for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to marshal international support for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to rein in the strong yen, likely leading to more pressure on the Bank of Japan to loosen monetary policy further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Koriki Jojima went into the annual gathering of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  and related events last week with a mission to convey Japan's discomfort with the yen's stubborn strength to his overseas counterparts--especially those from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of Seven\f28 \uc2 \f2  leading industrialized nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the outcries of Mr. Jojima and other Japanese officials over the yen's strength in the G-7 and bilateral meetings drew no convincing expressions of sympathy from foreign officials. With the U.S. and Europe offering no clear backing for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid their own economic woes, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government may keep out of the market and instead press the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen through more monetary loosening, analysts say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Pressure on the BOJ to act will likely increase" ahead of its next policy meeting Oct. 30, said Tohru Sasaki, chief researcher of Japanese bond and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets at JPMorgan Chase Bank in Tokyo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Sasaki said the effects of the recent U.S. monetary easing could possibly push the dollar down to near Y76.00 by year-end--well below the average level big Japanese manufacturers have factored into their business plans. The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stood at Y78.56 around 0815 GMT Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ already increased the size of its asset purchase program by Y10 trillion ($128 billion) to Y80 trillion last month by expanding its purchases of Japanese government bonds and short-term treasury bills.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet should the yen climb and make Japanese goods less competitive abroad, thereby threatening to exacerbate the current downturn of Japan's export-reliant economy, there is "a good chance" the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will act again as early as this month, said Yuji Kameoka, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Daiwa Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even if the BOJ refrains from acting, Mr. Kameoka said he expects it to implement additional monetary easing by the end of this year, possibly with a Y10-trillion increase in the asset-purchase ceiling.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the finance ministry is possible, the apparent lack of foreign endorsement of such action means it could draw complaints from abroad, something that tends to weaken the impact of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , analysts say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Jojima's grumbling over the yen and his calls against excessive exchange-rate volatility drew "no objection" during Thursday's meeting of G-7 finance chiefs, a Japanese government official has said. But there is no guarantee the argument often made by Japanese officials that "silence means approval" applies this time--given what happened at a meeting of leaders from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of 20\f28 \uc2 \f2  leading countries last November, and after that as well.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the G-20 summit, Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda told reporters that when he explained \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision to resume \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Oct. 31, "there were no comments" from them. Some traders took that to mean Japan had won approval for the action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Then in late December, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  stunned traders and the Japanese government by issuing a report harshly criticizing Tokyo's solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , rebutting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's contention that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market had been disorderly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nor is it clear how supportive the IMF is of potential Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . While IMF Managing Director \f28 \uc2 \f2 Christine Lagarde\f28 \uc2 \f2  told Mr. Jojima Thursday she considered the yen to be "moderately overvalued," in its report in August on U.S. policy, the fund used exactly the same phrase to characterize the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts also took note of last week's objections by Naoyuki Shinohara, one of Ms. Lagarde's senior deputies, to the idea of the BOJ buying foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such foreign-bond buying could be considered a form of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen. Thus, Mr. Shinohara's opposition suggests the IMF "isn't necessarily positive" about pushing the yen lower through sustained \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Daiwa Securities' Mr. Kameoka.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 15, 2012 04:50 ET (08:50 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121015e8af001yr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
