\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  GLOBAL CALL: Our Take on the Day's Big  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  News\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2012\par 13:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Global Call covers the main talking points from a news meeting involving DJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader editors in New York, London and Singapore, as well as other \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  hot spots when warranted.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By \f28 \uc2 \f2 Michael Casey\f28 \par \par \uc2 1. A quiet day all round in markets. Very little of any note for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has emerged out of the IMF/G-7 meetings in Tokyo beyond the usual narrative from Japanese officials on how their G-7 counterparts raised no objection to the idea of Japan acting unilaterally to weaken the yen. This has once again put traders into \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -watch mode. Such statements from the Finance Minister are a time-honored tradition after G-7 meetings--the newly hired Koriki Jojima merely demonstrated how occupants of that position are tightly bound by the traditions of the ministry--and are really pretty meaningless for what they say about international views on Japanese policy. We know that the U.S. is never a fan of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it's not likely to kick up a stink over it in this forum. Nonetheless, there is now a growing consensus that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar gets below Y77.50. The Japanese economy is looking weaker than it did the last time we were near these levels and exports in particular are under pressure. This would seem to raise the prospect of action.\par \par \uc2 2. China guided the yuan higher against the U.S. dollar to the strongest level in almost two decades, raising speculation that Beijing is offering a conciliatory gesture to Washington ahead of the U.S. presidential election. The alternative theory is that the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  was taking its lead from Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [See below]. Either way, such moves are indicative of how the Fed's quantitative easing program is expected to push the dollar lower against key Asian counterparts, if not against the euro.\par \par \uc2 3. That QE3-led flood of dollars around the world may be the motive for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore\uc2 's unexpected decision not to loosen policy, even after the country's third quarter GDP showed a bigger-than-expected 1.5% contraction from the second quarter. It suggests the MAS is worried about foreign liquidity boosting housing prices and lifting wages now that Singapore has clamped down on immigrant workers. The MAS's decision drove up the Singapore dollar but it also helped drive the Aussie dollar higher and others that moved in sympathy.\par \par \uc2 4. In the U.S. Friday we have the producer prices index at 8:30 a.m. EDT. It is expected to show an annualized gain in September of just 0.8%, sharply lower than the 1.7% reported for August. Food and energy prices are the driver in that expected shift, however. When they strip those out, economists see the "core" PPI showing a 0.2% rise, unchanged from the previous month. Nonetheless, such forecasts underscore how inflation continues to pose no real threat in actual data, giving the Fed cover to carry on its merry money-printing way.\par \par \uc2 5. We also get the preliminary read for U.S. October consumer sentiment from the University of Michigan and Reuters. Economists expect that index to come in at 78, down from late-October's 78.3.\par \par \uc2 6. The European Union won the Nobel Peace Prize. No comment.\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 Michael Casey\uc2  is managing editor for the Americas at DJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  news service jointly produced by Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal.)\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 12-10-12 1131GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121012e8ac000op\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
