\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: Japan Could be Backing Itself Into a Corner\b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2012\par 10:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Growth downgrades puts JPY strength back in spotlight\par \par \uc2 --G-7 show little support for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Unsuccessful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could make matters worse\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 The economic need for Japanese action may be getting even more urgent.\par \par \uc2 But the chances of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  getting the yen down are getting slimmer.\par \par \uc2 At the moment, the Japanese authorities are playing a game, hoping to convince financial markets that if they talk the yen down enough, they won't actually have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, this tactic will only succeed briefly.\par \par \uc2 As it becomes clear that Japan doesn't have support from any other G-7 countries and that any market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to be unilateral, yen buying will simply resume.\par \par \uc2 Japan remains in precisely the same fix it has been in for months.\par \par \uc2 The global slowdown is hitting its economy in two ways. Demand for Japanese exports is falling and, at the same time, support for the yen, because of its safe haven role, means the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains overvalued.\par \par \uc2 With the yen remaining in a strong Y78 to Y80 range against the dollar over the last five months or so, the economic toll is rising fast. For the third month in a row, the government has downgraded its assessment of the economy and new figures showed that total machinery orders plummeted by 12.6% in August.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is once again under fire for not easing monetary policy far enough and fast enough and the whole issue of yen strength is now very much back center stage.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials attending the latest \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  and G-7 meetings in Tokyo have certainly been touting for support from other countries to help get the yen down.\par \par \uc2 So far, though, there haven't been any takers. Most other major economies are suffering from the impact of the global slowdown as well. And, with the yen remaining in a fairly narrow range for months, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can hardly use the excuse that it needs to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of volatility.\par \par \uc2 Of course all the talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  coming out of the Tokyo meetings has been enough to weaken the yen a little for now.\par \par \uc2 But, the background flows that have been pushing the yen higher are unlikely to change.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  the latest weekly data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows that yen inflows from foreign investors into Japanese bonds and money market instruments totalled Y611 billion.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  itself argues that given the low yields offered by Japanese paper, these inflows are unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 How long they are sustained, though, will depend on how long safe havens remain in favor.\par \par \uc2 For the moment, there is little sign of market sentiment seriously changing.\par \par \uc2 And the longer that happens, the more tempted Japan will be to wade in to the market on its own. But, as past exercises in manipulating the yen have shown, unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can often not only be difficult but self-defeating.\par \par \uc2 If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  launches an unsuccessful exercise in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now it could well find that it is left with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could strengthen even further if investors feel that the authorities have had to give up.\par \par \uc2 (This is an opinion column by Nicholas Hastings, who is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires and has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493, by email at nick.hastings@dowjones.com or on Twitter @NickHastingsDJ) [ 12-10-12 0858GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1066 1074   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121012e8ac000j4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
