\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2012\par 02:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The euro rebounded Thursday, as investors digested the downgrade of Spain's credit rating and raised bets the move will put pressure on the Spanish government to finally seek a long-awaited bailout from the European Union. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at $1.2927 from $1.2875 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y78.35 from Y78.19, and at CHF0.9348 from CHF0.9391. The Wall Street Journal Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at about 69.797 from about 69.965. The U.K. pound was at $1.6044 from $1.6008.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by lower investor risk aversion; hopes that Spain's credit rating downgrade by S&P would hasten Spain to seek a bailout from the European Union, a move necessary to activate the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's broader bond-buying program; diminished worries over Greece after a senior finance ministry official said there is progress in talks Thursday between Athens and a visiting troika of international inspectors, adding that a deal on austerity measures Greece must take could be reached in time for the mid-October European summit; German Chancellor Merkel's hint of possible tax cuts to boost domestic demand in Germany because of Berlin's role "to do something for the stimulation of the economy in Europe". But EUR/USD gains tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0900 GMT euro-zone August industrial production. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turning bullish; bullish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Resistance at 1.2952 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.2991 (Tuesday's high), then 1.3035 (Monday's high), 1.3072 (Oct. 5 reaction high), 1.3085 (Sept. 19 high) and 1.3172 (Sept. 17 reaction high). Support at 1.2843 (hourly chart), then at 1.2825 (Thursday's low, near 200-day moving average); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 1.2803 (Oct. 1 low), then 1.2753 (Sept. 11 low) and 1.2655 (55-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by yen-funded carry trades amid decreased risk aversion (VIX fear gauge eased 4.3% to 15.59, although U.S. stocks closed narrowly mixed overnight with DJIA off 0.14%, S&P up 0.02%) as worries diminish over Europe's sovereign debt crisis. USD/JPY also supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy minister Maehara's comment that Tokyo may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market even without U.S.'s consent to cap yen's strength; demand from Japan importers. But USD/JPY gains tempered by Japan exporter sales; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 2350 GMT Japan August tertiary industry index, September money stock, September corporate goods price index, 1230 GMT U.S. September PPI, 1355 GMT U.S. October University of Michigan survey of consumers (preliminary), 1635 GMT Fed's Lacker speech. USD/JPY daily positive-biased as MACD in bullish mode, stochastics turning bullish; bullish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Resistance at 78.59 (Thursday's high); breach would target 78.76 (Monday's high, matching 100-day moving average), then 78.88 (Oct. 5 high), 79.23 (Sept. 19 high), 79.35 (200-day moving average) and 79.66 (Aug. 20 reaction high). Support at 77.94 (Thursday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 77.79 (Oct. 1 low), then 77.43 (Sept. 28 low), 77.13 (Sept. 13 low) and 76.50 (Feb. 6 low).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by waning investor risk aversion. But GBP/USD gains tempered by GBP sales on buoyant EUR/GBP cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turning bullish at oversold. Resistance at 1.6054 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.6142 (Monday's high), then 1.6217 (Oct. 5 high), 1.6272 (Sept. 28 high) and 1.6309 (Sept. 21 near-13-month high). Support at 1.5995 (hourly chart), then at 1.5982-1.5973 band (Thursday's low-Tuesday's low); breach would tilt near-term outlook negative, targeting 1.5957 (Sept. 10 low), then 1.5925 (55-day moving average), 1.5824 (Sept. 5 low) and 1.5801 (200-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by broadly weaker demand for safe-haven USD amid decreased risk aversion. But USD/CHF losses tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turning bearish. Support at 0.9332 (Thursday's low); breach would target 0.9318 (Tuesday's low), then 0.9291 (Monday's low), 0.9270 (Oct. 5 low) and 0.9235 (Sept. 14 reaction low). Resistance at 0.9418 (Thursday's high), then at 0.9431-0.9438 band (Wednesday's high-Oct. 1 high); breach would target 0.9473 (Sept. 11 high), then 0.9485 (Sept. 10 high) and 0.9580 (Sept. 7 high).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by receding investor risk aversion; stronger commodity and oil prices; sovereign demand for CAD. But USD/CAD losses tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD in bullish mode, but stochastics neutral; bearish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Support at 0.9759 (Thursday's low); breach would target 0.9740 (Tuesday's low), then 0.9731-0.9722 band (Oct. 5 low-Sept. 19 low), 0.9690 (Sept. 17 low) and 0.9630 (Sept.14 low). Resistance at 0.9834 (Thursday's high), then at 0.9858 (55-day moving average); breach would target 0.9884 (Oct. 3 reaction high), then 0.9918 (Sept. 5 high), 0.9931 (Aug. 30 high) and 0.9948 (Aug. 23 high).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by diminished investor risk aversion; reduced expectations that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  would cut its key rate at its next meeting on Nov. 6 after strong Australian September jobs data; firmer commodity prices; AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie; Aussie demand on buoyant AUD/NZD cross. But AUD/USD gains tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart positive-biased as stochastics rising from oversold; negative MACD histogram bars contracting. Resistance at 1.0293 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0375 (Oct. 2 high), then 1.0403 (Oct. 1 high), 1.0474 (Sept. 28 high) and 1.0520 (Sept. 21 high). Support at 1.0205 (Thursday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, targeting 1.0176-1.0171 (Wednesday's low-Tuesday's low), then 1.0145 (Monday's near-three-month low), 1.0096 (July 12 reaction low) and psychological 1.0000 line.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by subdued investor risk aversion; firmer commodity prices; NZD-USD yield gap. But NZD/USD gains tempered by Kiwi sales on buoyant AUD/NZD cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart still negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stay suppressed at oversold; though inside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Resistance at 0.8191 (Thursday's high), then at 0.8206 (Wednesday's high) and 0.8237 (Tuesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8265 (Oct. 5 high), then 0.8337 (Oct. 2 high), 0.8356 (Sept. 28 high) and 0.8470 (Feb. 29 reaction high). Support at 0.8148-0.8141 band (Thursday's low-Wednesday's low, near 55-day moving average); breach would tilt near-term outlook negative, exposing downside to 0.8068 (Sept. 11 low, near 200-day moving average), then 0.7998 (Sept. 7 low).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan rose against the U.S. dollar late Thursday to its highest closing level since Beijing revalued the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2005, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly guided the Chinese yuan stronger via a daily reference rate. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.2770 around 0830 GMT, lower than Wednesday's close of CNY6.2833. It traded in a range of CNY6.2761, the lowest level under the current system, to CNY6.2808. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3391, much lower than Wednesday's 6.3449, despite the dollar's steadiness in the overseas market overnight. Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts fell to 6.3744/6.3784 from 6.3944/6.3989 late Wednesday, implying a 1.8% fall by the yuan over the next year. In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar was at CNY6.2857, lower than CNY6.2920 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020121012e8ac00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
