\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Econ Min Maehara:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Alone Without US Consent\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Mochizuki and Alexander Martin  \par \uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2012\par 12:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy minister said Thursday that Tokyo may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on its own to rein in the yen's strength, even without the consent of the United States.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Seiji Maehara also said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an issue over which Japan and the U.S. were at odds, and their differences couldn't be resolved through negotiations.\par \par \uc2 "If we judge that the yen has become excessively strong, even if we have held prior talks, it's true that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own," Mr. Maehara said in an exclusive interview, when asked about U.S.-Japan relations over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue reflects national interests," Mr. Maehara said, adding: "It's not something to negotiate about."\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't Mr. Maehara's prerogative, his comments are another indication of Japan's concerns about the yen's persistent strength, which is hurting the country's already slowing exports.\par \par \uc2 Separately Thursday, another Cabinet member, Finance Minister Koriki Jojima, said he conveyed his worries over the yen's strength to his counterparts in the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  advanced industrialized economies, who met on the sidelines of the annual meeting of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Maehara, known as an advocate of aggressive monetary easing, will hold a rare meeting with Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 He said he won't raise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue with the Fed chairman, instead turning to the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , suggesting the BOJ's monetary easing so far hasn't been enough to beat long-running deflation.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ itself set up a 1% inflation goal in February, and currently prices are still falling moderately, meaning the goal hasn't been achieved yet. It's important that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  do what it can to fulfill its promise," Mr. Maehara said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Maehara repeated that foreign bond purchases are a policy option for the BOJ, something the finance ministry opposes, as it is seen as de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is under the ministry's mandate.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Maehara stressed that his idea is purely a way to expand the monetary base, adding that it's up to the BOJ to consider how to conduct it in a way that wouldn't be seen as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The former ruling party policy chief also said the government may seek a policy agreement with the BOJ should the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fail to deliver results in its fight against deflation.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Mochizuki at takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com and Alexander Martin at alexander.martin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\uc2  [ 10-11-12 0718ET ]