\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Finance Minister Voices Yen Concerns At G-7 Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2012\par 14:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Japanese finance minister tells G-7 of his worries over strong yen and desire for the group to reaffirm \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  principles\par \par \uc2 --A finance ministry official says other G-7 members express no objection to the minister's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  remarks\par \par \uc2 --The G-7 discussed the global slowdown and monetary policy, the minister says\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from Japan's finance minister in 6th, 10th paragraphs, remarks by a finance ministry official in 8th paragraph, background)\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi and Mitsuru Obe\par \par \uc2 TOKYO--Japan's finance minister made good on his promise Thursday to convey his worries over the yen's strength to his \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  counterparts, but there was little sign the rest of the club showed any sympathy toward Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  woes.\par \par \uc2 Comments by Finance Minister Koriki Jojima suggest the finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  heads of the G-7 leading industrialized countries instead spent much of their closed-door meeting debating issues such as the effects of U.S. monetary easing and the slowing global economy.\par \par \uc2 That could add to speculation the U.S. and Europe can hardly afford to support Japanese market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen and help the country's exporters at a time when their own economies need help from exports to emerge from a slump.\par \par \uc2 "I clearly explained that I have strong concerns about the yen's continued strength in the face of no bright fundamentals in our nation's economy, as well as the adverse effects of rises in the yen," Mr. Jojima said at a news conference after the latest G-7 meeting.\par \par \uc2 "I told them it is important to continue to share the group's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  view that we will closely consult with each other about actions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and cooperate as appropriate."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jojima added that he let U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Timothy Geithner\uc2  know during a separate one-on-one meeting that the yen's strength is a major risk to Japan's economy alongside China's slowing growth and Europe's debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Jojima was tight-lipped about whether the U.S., Germany, France, the U.K., Canada or Italy voiced any sympathy toward Japan's concerns, or agreed to reaffirm the G-7's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  principles.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Japan's economy minister admitted that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an issue over which Japan and the U.S. were at odds, and that Tokyo may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on its own even without U.S. consent.\par \par \uc2 "If we judge that the yen has become excessively strong, even if we have held prior talks, it's true that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own," Economy Minister Seiji Maehara said in an exclusive interview, when asked about U.S.-Japan relations over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't part of Mr. Maehara's mandate, he added that differences over \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't be resolved through negotiations.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue reflects national interests," Mr. Maehara said, adding: "It's not something to negotiate about."\par \par \uc2 A senior \uc2 Japanese finance ministry\uc2  official told reporters that the rest of the G-7 voiced "no objection" to Mr. Jojima's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remarks. But the official said other members just "passed them over" and didn't engage in any discussions on the yen's uptrend.\par \par \uc2 The development appears to be a rehash of previous international meetings, where Japan raised the issue of yen strength and met little reaction.\par \par \uc2 While the absence of verbal objection could be taken as a green light for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts have been skeptical that the U.S. would back such measures after the harsh criticism it made late last year over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Jojima declined to disclose comments made by other G-7 members during the gathering, he said there was "a very technical, deep, free and detailed discussion" on monetary policy. "There were discussions on under what circumstances monetary easing measures could be effective. Questions were raised about the policies of individual countries, especially about the U.S."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jojima said there was no talk on the latest territorial spat between Japan and China, which the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  now considers a potential risk to the world economy. But he acknowledged that the two countries' economic relations "are close and mutually dependent," and that keeping them smooth is important for both Asia the global economy.\par \par \uc2 He said Beijing has room for fiscal and monetary policy to achieve its 7.5% growth target.\par \par \uc2 Takashi Mochizuki and Alexander Martin contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com and Mitsuru Obe at mitsuru.obe@dowjones.com [ 11-10-12 1217GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121011e8ab000t2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
