\par\par \b \uc2 DJ INTERVIEW: China-Japan Spat Could Hurt World Economy, IMF Official Warns\b0\par\par\uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2012\par 07:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --IMF Deputy Managing Director Shinohara says any intensification in China-Japan dispute a risk to global economy\par \par \uc2 --Mr. Shinohara urges China, Japan to make efforts to resolve differences\par \par \uc2 --Mr. Shinohara says more BOJ easing likely needed to achieve 1% inflation goal\par \par \uc2 --He sees BOJ foreign debt-buying as a form of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi\par \par \uc2 TOKYO--The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  on Tuesday renewed its call for China and Japan to work out their territorial dispute as rising tensions between the world's second- and third-largest economies threaten to exacerbate an already-sharpening global slowdown.\par \par \uc2 The spat between the two nations over islands in the East China Sea has intensified since last month, sparking violent anti-Japan protests in China, dealing a blow to Japanese auto sales and discouraging travel between the two countries.\par \par \uc2 "If this problem spreads, that could be a risk to regional or global economic activities," IMF Deputy Managing Director Naoyuki Shinohara said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 "Japan and China are neighbors and share various economic interests," Mr. Shinohara said. "I am expecting the governments of both nations to make efforts soon to effectively solve this issue, and I believe that's what they will do."\par \par \uc2 The remarks add to signs that the dispute is no longer just a regional problem, and is now a source of concern for the global economy, which the IMF warned in its latest estimates is at risk of sliding back into recession. Territorial disputes are likely to be key topics on the sidelines of the annual meetings of the IMF and \uc2 World Bank\uc2  in Tokyo, where financial officials and central bankers from around the world are gathering, while the international community looks for prompt, peaceful solutions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shinohara said the current IMF economic forecast doesn't take into account a potential escalation in the China-Japan dispute. The IMF slightly cut its global growth forecast to 3.3% for this year and to 3.6% for 2013 in its World Economic Outlook report released Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Shinohara said he is "optimistic" China and Japan can reach a "mutually beneficial outcome."\par \par \uc2 Turning to Japan's slowing economy, Mr. Shinohara signaled his hopes the Bank of Japan will loosen monetary policy further and expand its asset-buying program to reverse growth-stunting deflation.\par \par \uc2 "There is a high probability that it will become necessary for the BOJ to take more monetary easing steps, including unconventional policy measures" to achieve its 1% inflation goal, he said. "It is desirable for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to further think about what can be done" in terms of "amounts" and "communication with the markets."\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Shinohara didn't sound especially supportive of an idea--championed by some Japanese lawmakers--of the BOJ buying foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated bonds.\par \par \uc2 "The idea of the BOJ buying foreign debt probably has two aspects: one is about expanding monetary easing, the other is about carrying out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." But \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  "already has tools to do both," with the Finance Ministry equipped to carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the BOJ to loosen monetary policy, he said.\par \par \uc2 In any case, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be used only when the market becomes "excessively volatile or disorderly," Mr. Shinohara said. "From that perspective, I feel that the current market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  framework is functioning well."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shinohara's comments may add to views that foreign countries could consider BOJ foreign debt purchases as de-facto \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and wouldn't be happy to see it happen often.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Mr. Shinohara said it is up to Spain to decide whether to tap Europe's new bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 "Spain isn't asking the IMF for financial help at this point," he said. "We are not working on any plans in this regard."\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 09, 2012 01:17 ET (05:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020121009e8a9000q0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
