\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Japan's New Finance Minister to Raise Strong Yen Issue at \uc2 G-7\b0\par\par\uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2012\par 12:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Japan's new finance minister says will raise strong yen issue at \uc2 G-7\par \par \uc2 --Points to downside risks to Japan economy amid strong yen, Europe crisis\par \par \uc2 --Says will consider opposition demands for spending cuts to end parliamentary gridlock\par \par \uc2 (Adds background in 6th-7th paragraphs, additional remarks in 9th-16th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Mitsuru Obe \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO--Japan's new finance minister said Wednesday he will raise the issue of the strong yen with his \f28 \uc2 \f2 Group of Seven\f28 \uc2 \f2  counterparts when they meet next week, adding that Tokyo remains committed to taking action against any excessive rises in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are considering raising the issue at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 G-7\f28 \uc2 \f2  meeting," said Koriki Jojima in a group interview referring to an upcoming gathering of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers. "The yen's recent one-way rise isn't reflective of Japan's economic fundamentals."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Jojima added that he would "take decisive steps against any excessive yen rises," echoing his predecessor Jun Azumi's commitment to take action when necessary as well as his desire to build support for Japan's stance on the yen among major nations. Mr. Jojima assumed his post on Monday after a Cabinet reshuffle, becoming Japan's fifth finance minister since the Democratic Party of Japan took power in September 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's fragile economic recovery faces multiple challenges, including the strong yen, falling exports to crisis-hit Europe, an economic slowdown in China and the looming risk of a government shutdown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I am aware of signs of downside risks to the economy," Mr. Jojima said. "The condition of the economy is at a stage where a very close look is required in considering the need for extra fiscal spending."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealing with the strong yen is one of the most urgent issues. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has risen almost 50% in nominal terms against the dollar in the last five years, severely damaging the competitiveness of electronics and other export industries that drive the nation's economic growth. While \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has occasionally \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to weaken the yen, this has at times generated criticism from other \f28 \uc2 \f2 G-7\f28 \uc2 \f2  nations, including the United States.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monetary policy is now attracting attention as a way to address the strong yen issue. Mr. Jojima's colleague, economic and fiscal policy minister Seiji Maehara, has called for the introduction of foreign asset purchases by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  to help weaken the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Jojima said he is looking for the BOJ to work in tandem with the government to support the economy through its monetary policy, while sounding cautious about the idea of allowing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to purchase foreign assets to help weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Ending deflation is the top priority for the government of Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda," he said. "We want the BOJ to take decisive steps as needed." But the idea of using monetary policy for stabilizing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market should be considered with caution, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Regarding the danger of a government shutdown, Mr. Jojima called on the ruling DPJ to hold talks with opposition parties in good faith so that discussions over a deficit-funding bond issuance bill can move forward. He indicated that the government could consider making concessions on spending commitments to help reach a deal with the opposition.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The DPJ-led government is seeking parliamentary approval for issuing bonds to fund the budget for the current fiscal year ending in March, but the opposition--which controls the upper house--has taken the issue hostage to press for a snap lower-house election and a reversal of some of the DPJ's spending pledges. Negotiations over the bill are expected to be fraught with difficulties, but Mr. Jojima stressed that the continued stalemate would only hurt the nation by raising the risk of a government shutdown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It would become necessary to carry out drastic measures" to make ends meet under such circumstances, Mr. Jojima said. "An agreement should be reached as soon as possible."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Jojima said the government is prepared to consider dropping some of the spending plans included in the fiscal 2012 budget to gain opposition support. "Our stance is to consider any proposals from the opposition seriously."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before becoming finance minister, Mr. Jojima helped broker a deal between the DPJ and the LDP to secure the passage of a sales tax-hike bill.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another task for Mr. Jojima is to make progress in discussions on tax and social security reform, a signature policy of Prime Minister Noda. The government is set to launch a national panel for drawing up a blueprint for reform that is expected to include cuts in social security expenditure, such as welfare benefits.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Jojima, a former union leader, said he hasn't formulated his own view on how to go about the social security reform. "It's something that should be discussed by the panel as well as within the DPJ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Mitsuru Obe at mitsuru.obe@dowjones.com [ 03-10-12 1045GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121003e8a3000jf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
