\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-UPDATE 1-Japan praises Europe reforms ahead of G7, \uc2 IMF\uc2 , urges sustained efforts\b0\par\par\uc2 936 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2012\par 15:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, comments from Japan's finance minister)\par \par \uc2 * Japan's top financial diplomat praises Europe's efforts, urges more\par \par \uc2 * Vice finmin Nakao optimistic about China growth, cooperation\par \par \uc2 * Japan to reiterate at G7 its view that yen too strong\par \par \uc2 By Tomasz Janowski and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The euro zone has made much progress in battling its three-year old debt crisis with the tough reforms taken by hardest-hit countries particularly encouraging and it needs to keep up the momentum, Japan's top financial diplomat said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 He also made conciliatory comments towards China over the dispute between the two countries over sovereignty of a small group of islands that has raised tensions between the two countries.\par \par \uc2 Takehiko Nakao, vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters in an interview that Japan will urge Europe to sustain the pace at a \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  finance chiefs' meeting and annual \uc2 IMF\uc2  and \uc2 World Bank\uc2  gatherings in Tokyo next week.\par \par \uc2 Japan has repeatedly voiced concern over the pace and scope of Europe's efforts, worried that the prolonged turmoil was boosting the safe-haven appeal of the yen and driving it to heights that the country's exporters struggled to cope with.\par \par \uc2 Nakao said the euro crisis remained one of the top challenges for the international community, but also struck a positive note.\par \par \uc2 "I think the euro zone has made a real effort and has made a lot of progress regarding the policy of the ECB, the rescue funds and stronger governance of the euro system regarding fiscal consolidation," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Above all, Greece and Spain and other countries are making very serious efforts of reform and fiscal consolidation."\par \par \uc2 "What I can say is that they should continue to take decisive action to implement things which would sustain and strengthen the economic and monetary union of Europe."\par \par \uc2 Spain, the euro zone's fourth-largest economy, is expected to seek international help to meet its financing needs after it passed a crisis budget last weekend.\par \par \uc2 But unlike in the past, Spain's rescue would be supported by\par \par \uc2 the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last month pledged to buy potentially unlimited amounts of bonds from governments that ask for help and agree to tough economic reforms.\par \par \uc2 Japan's newly appointed Finance Minister Koriki Jojima told reporters earlier on Wednesday he was likely to bring up Japan's views on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the G7 meeting.\par \par \uc2 Jojima reiterated Tokyo's long-held position that the yen, largely driven by overseas developments such as the euro crisis, was too strong for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic conditions and that Tokyo was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to correct that - a message echoed by Nakao.\par \par \uc2 Nakao said next week's discussions will also involve other risks to the world economy, such as a threat of a 'fiscal cliff' of expiring tax breaks and spending cuts in the United States and an economic slowdown in China and other emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Yet while China is seen heading for the seventh consecutive quarter of slowing growth in the third quarter, Nakao was optimistic about its longer-run growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 "They now feel difficulty because of the slowdown in exports to Europe and because of the impact of tightening in response to the inflation hit," he said. "But in the medium-term, China is still a developing country and many people want more things like cars and TV sets so I think that China can achieve high growth more based on domestic demand instead of external demand."\par \par \uc2 The ministry's top financial diplomat also said that despite a recent flare-up in tensions between the two countries over disputed East China Sea islands, he hoped the two top Asian economies would not abandon efforts to strengthen economic and financial ties.\par \par \uc2 Sino-Japanese relations deteriorated sharply after Japan in September bought the East China Sea islets that both Tokyo and Beijing claim, sparking anti-Japan protests across the country.\par \par \uc2 The disputed group of islands, called Senkaku in Japan and Diaoyu in China, are located near rich fishing grounds and potentially huge oil and gas reserves. Taiwan also asserts its own sovereignty over the islets.\par \par \uc2 Japan and China started trading their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in June as part of an agreement struck by the leaders of the two countries in December, which also involves Japan buying Chinese government debt.\par \par \uc2 "There's no signal that we would reverse. I have been in close communication with China and I hope we can continue to deepen cooperation in this area too," Nakao said.\par \par \uc2 Speaking about a meeting devoted to Myanmar that will be held on the sidelines of next week's gatherings at Tokyo's initiative, Nakao said it would give the nation's leaders a chance to convince creditors of their commitment to reforms and pave the way towards debt relief and large-scale economic assistance.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not expecting that this meeting is becoming a pledging session. This is still an early stage of a deepening of the dialogue and discussion between Myanmar and donor countries," he said.\par \par \uc2 "What we want to have is a clear message from Myanmar representatives that they will make further progress in political reform and economic reform and efforts to end internal conflicts."\par \par \uc2 After decades of military rule the impoverished country embarked on sweeping political and economic reforms, but badly needs debt forgiveness, investment and international assistance to move on.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-IMF/(INTERVIEW, UPDATE 1, PIX) | ABN | D | E | RBN | G | J | M | ABX | BNX | FUN | Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121003e8a3000t5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
