\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories of the Day\b0\par\par\uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2012\par 08:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: WEAK JAPANESE ECONOMIC DATA SHOWS GROWING RISK OF RECESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weak economic data released by the government Friday showed that the Japanese economy may be slowing more than expected amid headwinds from overseas, and could possibly move into a recession. (published 0324 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par *FRENCH 2Q FINAL GDP +0.25% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (published 0530 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FITCH: CHINA, INDIA LIKELY TO EXPAND AT SLOWER PACE THAN EXPECTED IN 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China and India's economies are likely to expand at a slower pace this year than previously expected, amid worsening prospects for global growth and reduced ability or willingness in the two countries to implement policy stimulus, Fitch Ratings said Friday. (published 0148 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: SOUTH KOREA OUTPUT FALLS FOR 3RD STRAIGHT MONTH IN AUG; PAVES WAY FOR RATE CUT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's factory output fell for a third consecutive month in August as strikes in the automobile sector and two typhoons slowed business activities, paving the way for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to deliver another policy rate cut. (published 0210 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: S KOREA CURRENT-ACCOUNT SURPLUS AT 4-MONTH LOW AS EXTERNAL WOE WEIGHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's current-account surplus fell to a four-month low in August from a record-high in July as weak export demand combined with seasonal factors, \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday. (published 0244 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN'S AUTO PRODUCTION UP 4.5% ON YEAR IN AUG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Production of cars, trucks and buses in Japan increased 4.5% on year in August, rising for the 11th consecutive month, as the government's buying incentives spurred demand of fuel-efficient vehicles. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN'S AUTO EXPORTS FALL 5.4% ON YEAR IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's exports of cars, trucks and buses declined 5.4% on year in August, falling for the first time in eight months, as the stubbornly strong yen makes it hard to export vehicles profitably. (published 0433 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WEAK AUSTRALIA CREDIT GROWTH FUELS RATE CUT CALLS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia recorded only a slight increase in credit growth in August from July, supporting calls for a cut in interest rates when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy-making board next meets on Tuesday, according to economists. (published 0317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER WANTS SUCCESSOR TO CONVEY YEN WORRIES DURING IMF, \f28 \uc2 \f2 G-7\f28 \uc2 \f2  MEETS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's outgoing finance minister said Friday he wants his successor to convey Tokyo's concerns over the yen's persistent strength during the upcoming \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  meetings, suggesting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's efforts to win support for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue. (published 0218 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ARGENTINA'S PRESIDENT DEFENDS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICIES, SKIRTS REELECTION CONTROVERSY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Argentine President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cristina Kirchner\f28 \uc2 \f2  defended her government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies on Thursday, saying the controversial measures are needed to ensure the country has sufficient hard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to pay its debts and import bill. (published 0427 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S DOWNGRADES VIETNAM ON BANK-RELATED WEAKNESSES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's Investors Service Friday cut Vietnam's credit rating to B2 from B1, citing risks that the government will have to pump money into the country's banking system, and the latter's limited ability to support the economy. (published 0532 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI AUG INDUSTRIAL PRODUCTION INDEX FALLS 11.32% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's industrial production index fell in August for a third straight month, the Ministry of Industry's Office of Industrial Economics said in a statement Friday. (published 0325 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI MINISTRY: LIKELY TO TRIM 2012 INDUSTRIAL PRODUCTION GROWTH TARGET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's Ministry of Industry said Friday that it is likely to trim its target for 2012 industrial production growth of 6%-7% after industrial output dropped by 5.37% during the first eight months of the year. (published 0502 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA TRADE DEFICIT LIKELY NARROWED IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia likely posted a trade deficit for the fifth consecutive month in August on weak demand from main export markets and a further dip in some commodities prices, though the deficit was likely to have narrowed from the preceding month. (published 0602 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA SEPTEMBER CPI LIKELY +4.60% ON YEAR, +0.26% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in Indonesia likely rose at a slightly faster pace in September from a year earlier as food prices remained elevated due to a drought in the U.S., but most economists think this won't lead the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to raise rates as inflation is still within its target range. (published 0428 GMT) [ 28-09-12 0603GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120928e89s0008e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
