\par\par \b \uc2 Monetary Easing Fails to Give Markets Direction\b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2012\par 12:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --BOJ joins ECB and Fed in providing more global liquidity\par \par \uc2 --Success of all this easing of monetary policy is questionable\par \par \uc2 --Still no sign of the fiscal reform that's needed\par \par \uc2 Throwing money at a problem has never been a solution.\par \par \uc2 And the world's three main central banks--as well as global financial markets--may soon find out little has changed in that respect.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has just joined the U.S. Federal Reserve and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in making its response to the global slowdown.\par \par \uc2 But, as with the other two, the likely success of Japan's easing of monetary policy is uncertain and the yen, the euro and the dollar will remain pretty much in a state of limbo.\par \par \uc2 Sure, the euro been lifted by the news two weeks ago that the ECB is planning to buy the government bonds of euro-zone debtors and lower their borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 Confirmation of this well-leaked initiative sent the euro hurdling over $1.30 for the first time in four months. But, two weeks on and as uncertainty over implementation of the policy grows, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally is wilting fast.\par \par \uc2 Not only is the German Bundesbank still publicly criticizing the program, accusing the ECB of "financing governments by printing banknotes", but the primary intended recipient of the bond-buying program, Spain, is still wriggling in its attempts to escape further fiscal discipline.\par \par \uc2 Until Spain caves in and makes a formal request, the whole success of the ECB's grand design will remain in serious doubt.\par \par \uc2 The initial announcement by the Fed last week that it was introducing a third round of quantitative easing to help hasten the improvement in the U.S. employment market was also greeted with a certain amount of relief.\par \par \uc2 At last, it too was responding to the global slowdown by, yes, throwing more money at it.\par \par \uc2 But enthusiasm for QE3 is also rapidly falling away as questions arise over how quickly the additional liquidity will actually help to boost the real economy and whether this additional dose of unlimited easing will have inflationary consequences as some in the financial markets now fear.\par \par \uc2 Put in the light of the fiscal cliff that the U.S. economy faces early next year if a new U.S. administration doesn't resolve key tax and spending issues immediately after November's presidential election, QE3 may have even less of a positive impact on the U.S. economy than many hoped.\par \par \uc2 And that brings us to the Bank of Japan and its somewhat surprising decision to make another Y10 trillion of asset purchases on top of the Y70 trillion it has made so far.\par \par \uc2 Again, the market's initial reaction appears to be: Phew!\par \par \uc2 But, the impact of this policy easing is also questionable.\par \par \uc2 Will it be enough to encourage more domestic investors to put their money abroad and reduce the recent strength of the yen?\par \par \uc2 Will it have an effect on Japanese growth, if the country's leading trading partner, China, continues to slow down?\par \par \uc2 Will the yen fall enough to prevent the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  from having to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Jane Foley, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 , sums up what could well be a common view: "Even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to allow fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to circulate though the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, it is likely to be difficult for the yen to soften against a tide of cheap dollars. We continue to see risk of dollar/yen dipping back to the 78 area on a one- to three-month view."\par \par \uc2 So, three monetary easings later and the world is even further awash with liquidity.\par \par \uc2 But there is little sign of the fiscal reforms in the three major economies that would inject some fresh confidence into their recoveries and give financial markets, and major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the direction they are still waiting for.\par \par \uc2 (This is an opinion column by Nicholas Hastings, who is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires and has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493, by email at nick.hastings@dowjones.com or on Twitter @NickHastingsDJ)\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 19, 2012 06:45 ET (10:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120919e89j002he\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
