\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2012\par 02:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The dollar was weaker against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday as markets absorbed the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's move to buy more mortgage-backed bonds. The yen traded traded at 78.31 to the dollar, from 77.48 Thursday in New York, its strongest close since Feb. 8. Late Friday, the euro was at $1.3132 against the dollar versus $1.2987 in New York Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The Australian dollar was at $1.0548 versus $1.0547 on Thursday. The pound was at $1.6210, from $1.6152 on Thursday. The Wall Street Journal Dollar Index, which measures the dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 68.980 from 69.201.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting four-month high of 1.3169 Friday. Underpinned by weak USD sentiment; positive investor risk appetite; diminished worries about the euro-zone sovereign debt crisis as ECB's revamped sovereign bond-buying program would effectively backstop short-term funding for struggling euro-zone countries, while euro-zone ministers indicated Friday they are willing to give Greece more time to catch up with its bailout program targets. But EUR/USD gains tempered by worries that the euro zone is heading back into recession. Data focus: 0800 GMT euro-zone July balance of payments, 0900 GMT euro-zone 2Q labour cost index, euro-zone July trade balance. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD bullish, stochastics stays elevated at overbought, 5- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at 1.3169 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.3284 (May 1 reaction high), then 1.3385 (March 27 high). Support at 1.3079 (hourly chart), then at 1.2980 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.2855 (Thursday's low), then 1.2815 (Wednesday's low), 1.2753 (Tuesday's low) and 1.2626 (Sept. 7 low).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate after hitting six-day high of 78.50 this morning. Liquidity thin in Asia as Japan markets closed today for holiday. USD/JPY buoyed by yen-funded carry trades amid improved investor risk sentiment (S&P up 0.4% Friday) due to combined effect of \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's QE3 and \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's revamped bond-purchase program. USD/JPY also supported by higher \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields after 0.6% rise in U.S. August CPI for largest on-month increase since June 2009, and stronger-than-expected rise in University of Michigan consumer sentiment index to 79.2 early this month from the 74.3 final reading for August (vs 74.0 forecast); sell-yen orders from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importers; risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite closure of Tokyo markets. But USD/JPY upside limited by buy-yen orders from Japan exporters; negative USD sentiment as Egan Jones lowered credit rating of U.S. to AA-minus from AA. Data focus: 1230 GMT U.S. September Empire State manufacturing survey. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turned bullish at oversold. Resistance at 78.50 (this morning's high); breach would expose upside to 79.04 (Sept. 6 high), then 79.37 (Aug. 22 high), 79.52 (Aug. 21 high) and 79.66 (Aug. 20 reaction high). Support at 77.87 (hourly chart), then at 77.46 (Friday's low) and 77.13 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 76.50 (Feb. 6 low), then 76.02 (Feb. 1 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting four-and-a-half month high of 1.6256 Friday. Underpinned by positive investor risk appetite; negative USD sentiment. But GBP/USD gains tempered by GBP sales on buoyant EUR/GBP cross. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; five- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at 1.6256 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.6301 (April 30 swing high), then 1.6455 (Aug. 29, 2011 high). Support at 1.6140 (Friday's low), then at 1.6079 (Thursday's low) and 1.6061 (Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.5981 (Tuesday's low), then 1.5957 (Sept. 10 low), 1.5920 (Sept. 7 low), 1.5880 (Sept. 6 low) and 1.5824 (Sept. 5 low).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting four-month low of 0.9235 Friday. Undermined by negative USD sentiment; broadly weaker demand for safe-haven USD as risk appetite improves. But USD/CHF losses tempered by CHF sales on buoyant EUR/CHF cross. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stay suppressed at oversold, five- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 0.9235 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9191 (May 8 low), then 0.9039 (May 1 reaction low). Resistance at 0.9356 (Friday's high), then at 0.9419 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9473 (Tuesday's high), then 0.9485 (Sept. 10 high), 0.9580 (Sept. 7 high), 0.9597 (Sept. 6 high) and 0.9608-0.9617 band (Sept. 5 high-Aug. 30 high).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to consolidate in higher range after hitting 13-month low of 0.9630 Friday. Weighed by positive investor risk appetite; negative USD sentiment; sovereign demand for CAD; buoyant commodity & oil prices. But USD/CAD downside limited by corporate demand for USD; CAD sales on buoyant EUR/CAD cross. Data focus: 1230 GMT Canada July international transactions in securities. Daily chart mixed as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; five- & 15-day moving averages falling; but bullish hammer candlestick pattern completed Friday. Resistance at 0.9722 (Friday's high), then at 0.9773 (Thursday's high) and 0.9788 (Sept. 10 high); breach would expose upside to 0.9834 (Sept. 7 high), then 0.9914-0.9918 (Sept. 6 high-Sept. 5 high), 0.9931 (Aug. 30 high) and 0.9948 (Aug. 23 high). Support at 0.9630 (Friday's low); breach would reinstate near-term negative bias, exposing downside to 0.9488 (Aug. 1, 2011 low), then 0.9403 July 26, 2011 swing low).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD to consolidate in lower range after hitting near-six-month high of 1.0624 Friday. Buoyed by positive investor risk appetite; negative USD sentiment; buoyant commodity prices (CRB spot index closed up 3.47 Friday at 320.92); AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie. But AUD/USD upside limited by Aussie sales on buoyant EUR/AUD cross. Data focus: 0130 GMT Australia August new motor vehicle sales. Daily chart mixed as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; five-day moving average above 15-day MA and rising; but bearish doji shooting-star candlestick pattern completed Friday. Support at 1.0524 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0420 (Wednesday's low), then 1.0314 (Tuesday's low), 1.0271 (Sept. 7 low) and 1.0162 (Sept. 5 reaction low)). Resistance at 1.0624 (Friday's high); breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, targeting 1.0637 (March 19 reaction high), then 1.0669 (Mach 8 reaction high) and 1.0856 (Feb. 29 swing high).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD to consolidate in lower range hitting six-month high of 0.8354 Friday. Supported by positive investor risk appetite; negative USD sentiment; buoyant commodity prices; NZD-USD yield gap. But NZD/USD upside limited by Kiwi sales on buoyant EUR/NZD cross. Daily chart mixed as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; five-day moving average above 15-day MA and rising; but bearish shooting-star candlestick pattern completed Friday. Support at 0.8279 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8189 (Thursday's low), then 0.8156 (Wednesday's low), 0.8068 (Tuesday's low) and 0.7998 (Sept. 7 low). Resistance at 0.8354 (Friday's high); breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, exposing upside to 0.8470 (Feb. 29 reaction high), then 0.8571 (Aug. 31, 2011 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan rose to its highest in more than four months against the U.S. dollar late Friday, as the greenback weakened sharply against the euro and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the U.S. Federal Reserve rolled out a new bond-buying plan overnight. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3145 around 0830 GMT, the lowest since May 11 when it closed at CNY6.3106, and also weaker than Thursday's close of CNY6.3296. It traded in a range of CNY6.3145 to CNY6.3228 Friday. Ahead of trading, the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3317, slightly lower than Thursday's 6.3338.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120917e89h0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
