\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: Calling the Dollar the Loser May Be Premature\b0\par\par\uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2012\par 11:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Economic impact of QE3 may not be so positive\par \par \uc2 --U.S. yields largely unchanged since QE3 announced\par \par \uc2 --Dollar's losses against other majors may well not last\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 The Fed has announced quantitative easing as we have never seen it before.\par \par \uc2 And the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reaction to it could be just as unpredictable.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's immediate response was as expected.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell--sharply--as the plans for unlimited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the mortgage-backed securities market raised the prospect of lower U.S. yields and reduced the attraction of traditional safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, as would be expected when another heavy dose of liquidity has been provided for the financial markets, equities rallied and commodity prices rose on the assumption that global demand will improve.\par \par \uc2 But those initial reactions could prove short-lived as financial markets continue to mull the impact of the Fed's actions.\par \par \uc2 For a start, \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2 's announcement of a $40 billion monthly dose of QE for as long as it takes to bring unemployment down was probably more than the market had bargained for. The depth of his dovishness could well be seen as alarming, given recent hopes that the U.S. economic recovery may be starting to accelerate.\par \par \uc2 Also, the whole program will be seen very much as the Fed's last chance to kick-start growth as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hardly likely to have any more ammunition even if events in the world economy, aka the euro-zone debt crisis, start to go wrong again.\par \par \uc2 However, it is the impact, or lack of it, that the program has on the U.S. economy that could well dictate how the dollar performs in the longer term.\par \par \uc2 By pledging unlimited purchases of mortgage-backed securities for the foreseeable future, the Fed is probably hoping to revive the housing market and provide a lift to the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 But it might just be encouraging already heavily-indebted U.S. consumers to take on more debt at the same time as the increase in monetary liquidity risks triggering inflationary pressures for the future.\par \par \uc2 And all this will be taking place as the U.S. economy continues to face the fiscal cliff that will appear next year when earlier tax breaks expire and knock consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 In other words, will this latest last-ditch attempt by the Fed to stimulate the economy be any more successful than QE1 and QE2?\par \par \uc2 If U.S. yields are anything to go by, the answer to that question is no.\par \par \uc2 Yields rose and then fell after the Fed announcement and are now sitting just about where they were before.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's fall against other higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as safe havens fell out of favor, was hardly surprising. Commodity and emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  all stand to benefit.\par \par \uc2 However, with easier monetary policy very much on the books for lower-yielding countries such as Japan, the euro zone and even the U.K., serious dollar losses against the other majors could prove short-lived.\par \par \uc2 Certainly the yen is already finding that rising against the dollar is a difficult business as speculation over either Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  easing or market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a difficult force to buck.\par \par \uc2 So the initial dollar reaction to the Fed's move late Thursday could well turn out to be just that--a knee-jerk move that doesn't reflect how the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will behave in the longer term.\par \par \uc2 (This is an opinion column by Nicholas Hastings, who is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires and has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493, by email at nick.hastings@dowjones.com or on Twitter @NickHastingsDJ) [ 14-09-12 0958GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1066 1074   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120914e89e000jy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
