\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-BOJ may mull easing as Fed move spurs yen; Tokyo threatens  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  action\b0\par\par\uc2 1,037 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2012\par 03:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Finmin says Tokyo will act decisively in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed\par \par \uc2 * Adds current yen moves don't reflect fundamentals\par \par \uc2 * Economics minister piles pressure on BOJ to act\par \par \uc2 * Govt cuts economic assessment for 2nd straight month\par \par \uc2 * BOJ to cut view on economy, exports and output\par \par \uc2 (Recasts with new lead, adds details on BOJ meeting)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 14 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will likely consider easing monetary policy next week to support its export-reliant economy, if yen rises persist following the U.S. Federal Reserve's stimulus measures, according to analysts and sources familiar with the BOJ's thinking.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also expected to offer a bleaker view of the economy than it did last month, when it said growth was starting to pick up moderately, as Europe's debt crisis and slowing demand in China weigh on exports, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts see an increased likelihood that the BOJ will act next week to tame a stubbornly strong yen, although it is hardly a foregone conclusion because of reluctance within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to use its limited policy options so soon.\par \par \uc2 The decision will likely be a close call. Bank policymakers are expected to scrutinise yen moves leading up to the two-day meeting, which ends on Wednesday, in reaching a decision on whether to take additional easing steps.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Jun Azumi repeated his warning to markets against pushing up the yen too much, saying authorities would take decisive action if necessary and won't rule out any measures - a thinly veiled threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Recent one-sided yen gains clearly do not reflect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic fundamentals," Azumi told a news conference on Friday.\par \par \uc2 With slowing global demand already clouding prospects of a near-term recovery, the BOJ is also more sensitive to the pain from a strong yen and may thus act pre-emptively to minimise the damage from yen rises on business sentiment, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 "The likelihood of the BOJ easing next week has heightened after the Fed's move," said Hideo Kumano, chief economist at Dai-ichi Life Research Institute in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "If the yen rises on Monday, when Japanese markets are closed for a national holiday, the BOJ may loosen policy in tandem with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government," he said.\par \par \uc2 If the BOJ were to act, its most likely option would be to expand its main monetary easing tool, a 70-trillion-yen ($904 billion) asset buying and loan programme, by 5 trillion or 10 trillion yen with most of the increase to be for purchases of government bonds, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 MOOD CHANGE POST-FED\par \par \uc2 In an escalated effort to boost growth, the Fed pledged on Thursday to buy $40 billion of mortgage debt per month and continue to purchase those and other assets until the outlook for jobs shows marked improvement.\par \par \uc2 The dollar briefly fell to 77.13 yen, its weakest since early February, on Thursday after the Fed's announcement, although it bounced back above 78 yen on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ had initially expected to hold off on loosening policy until next month, when it makes a more thorough analysis of whether Japan's economic recovery will be delayed enough to warrant further stimulus.\par \par \uc2 But the mood changed after the Fed revealed its aggressive asset-buying plan. The government, which cut its assessment of the economy for the second straight month, pressured the BOJ to expand stimulus to counter growing headwinds such as a stubbornly strong yen and slowing global demand.\par \par \uc2 "Overseas developments must be taken into account in guiding economic policy, including monetary policy," Economics Minister Motohisa Furukawa told a news conference when asked whether the BOJ should follow the Fed and ease policy next week.\par \par \uc2 Azumi also said he hoped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would "respond as appropriate" to signs of economic weakness, taking into account the government's economic assessment.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bond prices rose on Friday due to growing expectations the BOJ would follow the Fed's steps with some stimulus action of its own.\par \par \uc2 BOJ UNDER HEAT\par \par \uc2 The world's third-largest economy expanded less than expected in the second quarter and analysts now expect growth to stall for the rest of this year as Europe's debt crisis and subdued Chinese growth cloud the outlook.\par \par \uc2 Akio Toyoda, head of Japan's automobile lobby, called for bolder government and BOJ action to stem yen rises, warning on Friday that if the yen stayed too strong, companies may no longer be able to cover the pain with cost cuts.\par \par \uc2 But while policymakers repeatedly threaten \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tokyo has refrained from stepping into the market after spending 9 trillion yen in unilateral action late last year.\par \par \uc2 Market players say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be able to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again easily given the international criticism it provokes, making further monetary easing the more likely option.\par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor \uc2 Masaaki Shirakawa\uc2  already faced calls for bolder action in a meeting to discuss the government's monthly economic report, with one cabinet minister urging the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to opt for a big, Fed-style stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa declined to comment on what the BOJ could do next week, only replying that it would guide policy appropriately, according to a government official present at the meeting.\par \par \uc2 But he told business leaders last month that yen rises posed a "big negative impact on the economy", in a sign of how mindful the BOJ was of the pain from a strong yen.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ set a 1 percent inflation target and loosened policy via an increase in asset purchases in February, and followed up with another easing in April. It has stood pat since then.\par \par \uc2 Even if it holds fire next week, the BOJ is expected to offer a bleaker view of the economy than last month, and cut its assessment on exports and output, keeping alive expectations of near-term easing.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 77.4400 yen)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto, Kaori Kaneko and Yoko \uc2 Kubota\uc2 ; Editing by Michael Watson and Simon Cameron-Moore)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 3) | ABN | D | E | RBN | J | M | NAT | ABX | BNX | FUN | U | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120914e89e0001d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
