\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Recent yen moves don't justify  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  - ex-BOJ deputy Muto\b0\par\par\uc2 709 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2012\par 12:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Recent yen moves stable - ex-BOJ deputy governor\par \par \uc2 * Hard for BOJ to buy foreign bonds and call it easing\par \par \uc2 * BOJ's price f'casts too optimistic, may be revised down\par \par \uc2 * BOJ can buy more risk assets, longer-dated govt bonds\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 11 (Reuters) - A strong yen is among key risks to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy but Tokyo cannot justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market now because recent yen moves have been relatively stable, former Bank of Japan Deputy Governor Toshiro Muto said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Muto, also a former top finance ministry bureaucrat who retains strong influence in economic policy-making, said that yen rises may accelerate if renewed market fears over Europe's sovereign debt crisis boost appetite for the safe-haven Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But he added that recent yen moves have been stable and Japanese policymakers should not target specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels in deciding whether to step into the market to stem yen rises.\par \par \uc2 "At present, we're not seeing excessive volatility, so it's difficult to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market)," Muto told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has been hovering around 78 yen recently, well above its record low of 75.311 yen hit in October last year. But it briefly touched a six-week low of 77.99 yen on trading platform EBS on Tuesday amid broad dollar weakness.\par \par \uc2 Muto, regarded as a strong candidate to succeed BOJ Governor \uc2 Masaaki Shirakawa\uc2  when his term expires in April next year, dismissed calls by some lawmakers urging the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy foreign bonds.\par \par \uc2 "Those who call on the BOJ to buy foreign bonds mention \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. The BOJ won't be able to gain market trust if it buys foreign bonds to influence \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and yet describes the move as monetary easing," he said.\par \par \uc2 In Japan, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy falls under the ambit of the finance ministry, not the BOJ, which acts as an agent when Tokyo decides to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to stem yen rises.\par \par \uc2 Now chairman of private think-tank, \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2 , Muto was Japan's top financial bureaucrat before serving as deputy BOJ governor for five years until 2008.\par \par \uc2 With the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holding a policy-setting meeting next week, Muto said he saw little need for the BOJ to ease monetary policy immediately.\par \par \uc2 But he criticised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's price forecasts as too optimistic and said it may come under pressure to expand monetary stimulus soon if risks to Japan's economy heighten.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ may revise down its economic and price forecasts at its twice-yearly outlook report due out at the end of October. There's a chance it will also debate easing policy, judging that it will take longer to achieve 1 percent inflation," he said.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ set a 1 percent inflation target and loosened policy via an increase in asset purchases in February. It followed up with another easing in April but has held fire since then.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said a moderate economic recovery will help Japan reach close to 1 percent inflation sometime in the fiscal year beginning in April 2014.\par \par \uc2 But given uncertainties over the outlook, many analysts agree with Muto that the BOJ may cut its growth forecasts in its semiannual report on Oct. 30, and admit that it will take longer than expected to achieve 1 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 Japan has been mired in more than a decade of grinding deflation, which hurts corporate and household appetite to spend. Core consumer prices fell 0.3 percent in July from a year earlier, marking the third straight month of declines.\par \par \uc2 Muto said that while the BOJ is already doing what it can to support the economy, there is more it can do such as increasing purchases of risk assets or longer-dated government bonds.\par \par \uc2 In a country like Japan, where the risk of sudden inflation is small, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has room to take unorthodox policy steps to stimulate the economy and beat deflation, he said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Yoshifumi Takemoto; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (INTERVIEW)(PIX) | ABN | D | E | RBN | J | M | ABX | BNX | FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120914e89b000m1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
