\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Dips As Traders Wait On German Court, Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anusha Shrivastava   \par \uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2012\par 23:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The euro fell against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday as a series of recent gains were halted by some trepidation ahead of a German constitutional court decision on the euro zone's bailout fund and a meeting of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  later in the week.\par \par \uc2 At its meeting, which ends on Thursday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could initiate another round of asset purchases or it may simply extend the guidance on how long interest rates are kept low. Given the broad ramifications of the Fed decision, investors are largely sitting tight with volumes low across several markets.\par \par \uc2 "Right now the market is in a wait-and-see mode as traders watch for the decision of the German constitutional court...and the Fed's decision on another round of bond-buying," said Robert Fullem, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Mizuho \uc2 Corp. Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Market participants largely expect the German court to approve the constitutionality of the bailout fund. With such a decision, the euro could move a little bit higher, said Alvise Marino, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Longer term, however, there is likely to be increased downward pressure on the euro, with \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  forecasting the euro will be at $1.17 in three months and at $1.19 in 12 months.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the euro was at $1.2759 against the dollar versus $1.2815 in New York Friday, according to EBS via CQG. On Thursday, the euro rose to a two-month high versus the dollar, boosted by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  outlining a bond-buying plan to help euro-zone countries.\par \par \uc2 The euro was at Y99.86 against the yen, from Y100.30.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's fate in the short term is linked closely to whether there is further bond-buying--or quantitative easing--from the Fed, which would increase the supply of money in the system and weaken the greenback.\par \par \uc2 "The market is expecting meaningful easing this week and if it doesn't happen, the dollar will rally," said Alessio de Longis, a portfolio manager at Oppenheimer Funds. "If we don't get QE, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that suffer the most will be the yen and the euro."\par \par \uc2 If the bond-buying program is launched, Mr. de Longis said the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could rise to $1.30, which he sees as a "reasonable" target. If not, it would sell off to $1.2650.\par \par \uc2 The launch of more quantitative easing from the Fed would also lead to gains for the yen, though Japanese leaders could then step in to prevent further strengthening, Mr. de Longis said.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with negatively skewed payout is the yen. You get weakness either way, but the catalyst will be different," he said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was at Y78.28 on Monday, versus Friday's Y78.28.\par \par \uc2 The pound was at $1.5989, from $1.6001 on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Dollar Index, which measures the dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , closed the session at 70.205 from 70.011.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anusha Shrivastava at anusha.shrivastava@wsj.com [ 09-10-12 1700ET ]\par \par \uc2