\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: Trade Data Could Spell End To Yen Strength\b0\par\par\uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2012\par 11:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Data suggest both domestic and overseas demand falling\par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  may not be able to stimulate economy on its own\par \par \uc2 --U.S., China and euro zone still seen dragging their feet\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 While the world dithers, Japan burns.\par \par \uc2 And the yen, that once great safe haven, will grow less attractive.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is certainly one way to look at it as the latest trade figures from Tokyo show Japanese exports to two key regions, the euro zone and China, are collapsing.\par \par \uc2 Its imports, meanwhile, fell only modestly but those figures were helped a sharp increase in energy purchases.\par \par \uc2 It means both domestic and export demand for Japanese goods are falling at the same time.\par \par \uc2 Of equal importance for Japanese policymakers, was a decline in both import and export prices. The former can be understood given the strength of the yen itself. But the latter appears to have been driven by producers cutting prices in the hopes of maintaining their share of the overseas market.\par \par \uc2 Price cutting like that is unlikely to be sustainable in the long run. It will also increase deflationary pressures, cut company profits and suppress Japan's economic activity even further.\par \par \uc2 So the data increase pressure on the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to address the strength of the yen and ease monetary policy even further.\par \par \uc2 But, as many previous attempts to do so have shown, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is having trouble getting the export-reliant economy to recover on its own. Revival needs the global economy to improve too -- and, there is little sign of that happening just yet.\par \par \uc2 Despite talk in the U.S., the euro zone and China that further monetary stimulation is in the offing, there is no sign of action.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., a slight improvement in economic data could provide the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  with an excuse to wait until after the November presidential elections to ease policy again.\par \par \uc2 Similarly in China, new data showing house prices rising will give Beijing a good reason to delay its plans for further monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 Further easing in the euro zone is also up in the air. The region's imports from Japan have plummeted by over 25% in the last year, with potentially the most toxic impact on the Asian country.\par \par \uc2 With a Greek exit from the euro still possible and with European politicians groping their way towards a solution to prevent Spain and Italy also toppling, any attempts by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stimulate growth are hardly likely to have a major impact just now.\par \par \uc2 So what does this all this mean for the yen? The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been range bound against the dollar for most of the summer.\par \par \uc2 The yen was supported on one side by its safe-haven role in an uncertain world and by yield differentials between Japan and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 On the other side, it was prevented from going too far by fears of \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If anything, those fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are only likely to increase.\par \par \uc2 However, support for the yen could start to wane in a world where the Fed appears to be a little more hawkish, driving yield differentials back up in favor of the dollar, and where renewed deflationary pressures undermine confidence in Japan's economic prospects even further.\par \par \uc2 Then the dollar, which has failed to fall much below Y78 or managed to rally much above Y80.50 for several months, could make a more decisive break for the upside as investor interest in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes a distinct turn for the worse.\par \par \uc2 (Nicholas Hastings is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires who has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com or on twitter @NickHastingsDJ)\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 22-08-12 0934GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1066   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120822e88m000kr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
