\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Even More Likely Now\b0\par\par\uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2012\par 14:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Investor flows into yen likely to continue despite weak economy\par \par \uc2 --Sales tax could increase pressure for a weaker yen\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 The chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen are rising, even though neither the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  nor the Japanese government appear ready to take steps to boost the country's very fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 There are two reasons for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. The first is that its safe haven role has grown since the global financial crisis began five years ago, intensifying as the euro-zone sovereign debt crisis deepens and spreads.\par \par \uc2 Global sentiment and investor confidence in the ability of European politicians and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to resolve the issue without bringing an end to the euro itself have waxed and waned over the months.\par \par \uc2 The most recent hopes that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a "big bazooka" to contain the crisis have been dashed. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  raised expectations when he implied monetary policy makers were prepared to start buying the bonds of peripheral debtor countries peripheral only to follow up last week at the monthly board meeting with a climb-down.\par \par \uc2 The other reason the yen is finding support, especially against the dollar, is U.S. monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 As the U.S. recovery continues to splutter, expectations of further quantitative easing have increased, helping to drive U.S. yields lower and make the yen even more attractive against its U.S. counterpart.\par \par \uc2 Back in Tokyo, meanwhile, there appears to be little to counter these inflows.\par \par \uc2 Although the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is currently holding its latest two-day policy meeting and although the country remains at risk from another dose of deflation given that CPI fell by 0.1% over the last year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't expected to ease policy any more at this stage as it remains cautious about inflationary risks in the future.\par \par \uc2 The government isn't about to help either.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda has just reached an agreement with opposition leaders that should clear the way for the introduction of a key sales tax increase.\par \par \uc2 As this fiscal reform will likely increase pressure on Japanese consumers and make the economic recovery even more difficult, the need for the government to cap the yen's rise and help Japanese exporters will become even more imperative.\par \par \uc2 For most of the summer the yen has remained in a fairly narrow range against the dollar, hemmed in by expectations that if it rises too far the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ask the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stop it.\par \par \uc2 But, once the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting is over and once the sales tax is in place, and given that investor interest in the yen is only likely to grow, especially given that it is one of the few highly liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  available, chances are that the yen will start to rise again and make it more likely that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will have to move.\par \par \uc2 (Nicholas Hastings is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires who has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com or on twitter @NickHastingsDJ)\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 08-08-12 0832ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020120808e8880003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
