rters; wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; positions adjustment before weekend. Other data: 1400 GMT U.S. July ISM non-manufacturing report on business. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as stochastics bullish at oversold; negative MACD histogram bars contracting; but five-day moving average meandering sideways below falling 15-day moving average. Support at 78.11 (Thursday's low), then at 77.90 (Wednesday's two-month low); breach would expose downside to 77.65 (June 1 low), then 77.35 (Feb. 14 low) and 76.02 (Feb. 1 reaction low). Resistance at 78.54 (Thursday's high, matching Monday's high), then at 78.68 (July 27 high); breach would target 78.82 (July 19 high), then 79.17 (July 17 high), 79.40 (July 13 high) and 79.97 (July 12 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting four-week high of 1.2406 Thursday, as markets await U.S. non-farm payrolls report. Euro fell broadly Thursday as ECB President Draghi, after pledging last week he would do "whatever it takes" to safeguard the euro, disappointed by not taking immediate action to ease the euro-zone debt crisis such as cutting rates, reviving its sovereign bond-buying program, implement a third round of LTRO or awarding a bank license for the European Stability Mechanism. EUR/USD also undermined by worries that Spain may need sovereign bailout and that Greece might exit the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc; concerns that the euro zone is heading into recession. But EUR/USD losses tempered by positions adjustment as traders trim exposure ahead of weekend. Data focus: 0800 GMT euro-zone July services PMI, 0900 GMT euro-zone June retail sales. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish; but stochastics in bearish mode, bearish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Support at 1.2133 (Thursday's low), then at 1.2117 (July 26 low); breach would expose downside to 1.2052-1.2042 band (July 25 low-July 24 two-year low), then psychological 1.2000 line, 1.1876 (June 7, 2010 low), 1.1826 (Feb. 27, 2006 low) and 1.1640 (Nov. 15, 2005 low). Resistance at 1.2300 (hourly chart), then at 1.2406 (Thursday's high); breach would target 1.2425 (55-day moving average), then 1.2478 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of decline from June 18 high of 1.2748 to July 24 low of 1.2042) and 1.2538 (July 5 high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting four-month high of 1.0578 Thursday, as markets await U.S. non-farm payrolls report. AUD/USD undermined by negative risk sentiment; weaker commodity prices (CRB spot index closed down 4.73 Thursday at 294.5); Aussie sales on soft AUD/NZD cross. But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0200 GMT Australia July VFACTS vehicle sales. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish; but stochastics bearish at overbought; bearish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Support at 1.0431 (Thursday's low), then at 1.0380 (July 27 low); breach would expose downside to 1.0287 (July 26 low), then 1.0173 (July 25 low), 1.0118 (July 13 low) and 1.0096 (July 12 low). Resistance at 1.0504 (hourly chart), then at 1.0578 (Thursday's high); breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, targeting 1.0637 (March 19 high), then 1.0669 (March 8 high).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting three-month high of 0.8171 Thursday, as markets await U.S. non-farm payrolls report. NZD/USD undermined by negative risk sentiment; weaker commodity prices. But NZD/USD downside limited by NZD-USD yield gap; Kiwi demand on soft AUD/NZD cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish; but stochastics bearish at overbought; bearish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Support at 0.8064-0.8063 (Thursday's low-Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7985 (July 27 low), then 0.7883 (July 26 low), 0.7797 (July 25 low), 0.7745 (June 14 low) and 0.7712 (June 13 low). Resistance at 0.8125 (hourly chart), then at 0.8171 (Thursday's high); breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, exposing upside to 0.8234 (April 30 high), then 0.8318 (April 13 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting one-week low of 1.5488 Thursday, as markets await U.S. non-farm payrolls report. Bank of England Thursday held its policy rate at 0.5% and its bond purchase program at GBP375 billion as widely anticipated. GBP/USD undermined by negative risk sentiment. But GBP/USD losses tempered by GBP demand on weaker EUR/GBP cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0700 GMT U.K. July Halifax house price index, 0830 GMT U.K. July CIPS services PMI, 0830 GMT U.K. July official reserves. Daily chart negative-biased as stochastics bearish; MACD staged bearish crossover against its exponential moving average; five-day moving average staging bearish crossover against 15-day MA. Support at 1.5488 (Thursday's low); breach would target 1.5468 (July 26 low), then 1.5456 (July 25 reaction low), 1.5390 (July 12 reaction low) and 1.5320 (June 5 low). Resistance at 1.5606 (hourly chart), then at 1.5678 (Thursday's high) and 1.5690 (Wednesday's high); breach would target 1.5729 (Tuesday's high), then 1.5752 (Monday's high), 1.5765-1.5777 band (July 27 high-June 20 reaction high, near 100-day moving average and 50% Fibonacci retracement of decline from April 30 high of 1.6301 to June 1 low of 1.5265) and 1.5848 (May 22 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting one-week high of 0.9898 Thursday, as markets await U.S. non-farm payrolls report. USD/CHF supported by broadly stronger demand for safe-haven USD amid negative risk sentiment; \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as EUR/CHF hovers just above 1.2000 floor (cross last at 1.2012). But USD/CHF gains tempered by CHF demand on EUR/CHF cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish; but stochastics in bullish mode, bullish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Resistance at 0.9898 (Thursday's high); breach would target 0.9911 (July 26 high), then 0.9965-0.9972 band (July 25 high-July 24 19-month high), psychological 1.0000 line and 1.0066 (Dec. 1, 2010 reaction high). Support at 0.9773 (hourly chart), then at 0.9695-0.9690 (Thursday's low-July 27 low); breach would expose downside to 0.9665 (55-day moving average), then 0.9629 (61.8% retracement of advance from June 18 low of 0.9417 to July 24 high of 0.9972) and 0.9576 (July 5 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting 2.5-month low of 0.9999 Thursday, as markets await U.S. non-farm payrolls report. USD/CAD underpinned by negative risk sentiment; softer commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled down $1.78 Thursday at $87.13/bbl). But USD/CAD gains tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 1215 GMT Canada July official international reserves. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish; but stochastics bullish at oversold; bullish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Resistance at 1.0085 (Thursday's high); breach would target 1.0105 (July 27 high), then 1.0166 (July 26 high), 1.0231 (July 25 reaction high) and 1.0249 (July 12 reaction high). Support at 1.0022 (hourly chart), then at 0.9999 (Thursday's low); breach would reinstate near-term negative bias, targeting 0.9986 (May 15 low), then 0.9949 (May 11 low), 0.9920 (May 8 low) and 0.9825 (May 3 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting one-week low of 94.93 Thursday, as markets await U.S. non-farm payrolls report. EUR/JPY undermined by negative risk sentiment; Japan exporter sales. But EUR/JPY losses tempered by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand; wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish; but stochastics in bearish mode, bearish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Support at 94.93 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 94.66 (July 26 low), then 94.12 (July 24 near-12-year low), psychological round-numbered levels of 94.00 down to 92.00, then 91.35 (November 2000 low). Resistance at 96.17 (hourly chart), then at 96.99 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 97.33-97.38 band (July 27 high-July 17 high), then 97.90 (July 12 high), 98.13 (July 9 high) and 98.34 (55-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 02-08-12 2331GMT ]\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to consolidate after hitting three-week high of 0.7911 Thursday. Daily chart mixed as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode, five-day moving average staged bullish crossover against 15-day MA; but bearish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday. Support at 0.7822 (Thursday's low), then at 0.7796-0.7787 band (Tuesday's low-Monday's low), 0.7765 (July 24 low) and 0.7753 (July 23 45-month low); breach would expose downside to 0.7692 (Oct. 20, 2008 reaction low), then 0.7388 (Jan. 28, 2008 low). Resistance at 0.7911-0.7913 (Thursday's high-July 13 high); breach would target 0.7934 (July 10 high), then 0.7948 (July 9 high) and 0.7983 (July 6 high).\par \par \uc2 Write to Jerry Tan at jerry.tan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors whic