\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\uc2  Unlikely Unless USD Falls to Y76 -\uc2 UBS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2012\par 19:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:27 EDT - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen strength unless the dollar falls below Y76, says Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Global political pressure against \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also acts as a deterrent for the BoJ since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has come under fire for being a "serial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervener\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," suggesting the BoJ may choose to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by expanding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quantitative easing program instead of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly, he says. Jalinoos expects the yen to rally if the Fed and the ECB hint at further easing at their respective policy meetings this week. The dollar last trades at Y78.22, down from Y78.44 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. (nicole.hong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:22 EDT - Miner \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  (AAL.LN) won't be liable to pay fines on late start-up of its iron-ore shipping \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Acu port in Brazil until after July 2014, port company LLX (LLXL3.BR) clarifies. Anglo's recent postponement of the start up of its Minas-Rio iron ore project in Brazil by one year to 2H14 is still (just) within the timetable established under a "take-or-pay" accord struck in December 2010 with the port operating company, LLX says. (diana.kinch@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:17 EDT - Front-month natural-gas futures briefly reached $3.20/MMBtu as forecasts of extreme heat boost prices to yet another 2012 high. The September Nymex contract didn't get to $3.20 last week as the market gained every day. But above-normal temperatures expected to cover the eastern 2/3 of the US the next couple weeks is supporting demand for gas-powered electricity to fuel air conditioning. Not to be forgotten is the heart of hurricane season fast approaching; storms could threaten offshore natural-gas production. September futures are up 5.6% at $3.183. (nicole.friedman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:14 EDT - Venezuela President Hugo Chavez says among his plans at tomorrow's Mercosur gathering in Brasilia will be a meeting with the head of Brazilian oil giant \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  (PBR PETR4.BR) "to strengthen the energy alliance" between both countries. He also says he's looking to further ties with recently nationalized Argentine oil and gas company YPF (YPF). In comments carried on state television, he adds that new commercial jets from Brazil's Embraer (ERJ EMBR3.BR) will soon be incorporated into the fleet of Venezuela's state airline. In January, the president said Venezuela would purchase 20 jets. (kejal.vyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:07 EDT - After getting carried higher Thursday and Friday amid the broader stock market's surge, shares of Destination Maternity (DEST) are more than undoing those gains as Needham weighs in today with a downgrade of the maternity-apparel retailer to buy from strong buy, $3 price-target cut to $21 and cut expectations for the next 2 years. DEST issued a below-consensus 4Q EPS target on Thursday, following up its 3Q warning from earlier this month. Shares, down 2.2% today at $18.13, have slid 16% this month. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 13:05 EDT - Sifting through data on Colombian cocaine production has never been easy--it's an illegal industry, so satellite images and much guesswork is involved. Making matters worse, the US government and the UN have new, dueling reports that don't match up. The US Drug Czar's office said today "potential cocaine production" in Colombia fell 25% in 2011 vs 2010, to 195 metric tons, and that Colombia is now 3rd behind Peru and Bolivia in cocaine output. But the UN said last week Colombia's coca crops increased last year and said production of cocaine remained mostly steady. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:57 EDT - Apple (AAPL) shares are up amid more iPhone and iPad rumors. The blog iMore, citing sources, says AAPL would hold a launch event Sept 12 for the new phone and a mini iPad, with the iPhone 5 release coming nine days later. That, plus news of strong sales of AAPL's new Mountain Lion computer software clearly have investors rather happy. AAPL up 2% to $596.88. (ian.sherr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 Read more here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.imore.com/apple-iphone-5-and-ipad-mini-event-planned-september-12-iphone-5-release-date-september-21"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.imore.com/apple-iphone-5-and-ipad-mini-event-planned-september-12-iphone-5-release-date-september-21}}}\par \par \uc2 12:55 EDT - Teen retailer Hot Topic (HOTT) is continuing its recent stock rebound ahead of Wednesday's F2Q sales report. Janney, which has an above-consensus view on what the numbers will be and says an EPS-forecast raise from HOTT is plausible, says the company is poised to enter the back-to-school season "in a clean-inventory position," having reduced clearance merchandise. It adds HOTT also likely benefited from a strong movie release season as its stores carry merchandise tied to comic-hero blockbusters like May's "The Avengers" and this month's "The Amazing Spider-Man" and "The Dark Knight Rises." Shares rise 3.9% to $10.12, putting 2012's jump at 53%. (anna.prior@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:46 EDT - According to \uc2 Greenwich Associates\uc2 ' 2012 ranking, DB claimed the top spot with 12.2% market share in secondary fixed-income trading volume. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  took second, with 11.2% share. These two dealers also placed first and second in US rates trading, whereas \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  seated first in emerging-market debt. Overall, Greenwich found that the new capital rules have caused dealers as a group to "reduce the amount of capital devoted to fixed income and to pare back inventory held for trading." (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:41 EDT - Warner Bros subsidiary \uc2 New Line Cinema\uc2  and \uc2 Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer\uc2  had pretty much a sure bet on their hands with two films based on JRR Tolkien's "The Hobbit" due this year and next, but more could turn out to be merrier. Director Peter Jackson, returning to the mega-blockbuster world of his Oscar-winning "Lord of the Rings" trilogy, has fleshed out the relatively small "Hobbit" story to warrant a third film--and potentially hundreds of millions of dollars more in grosses. The first film "Hobbit" film is due this Dec, with the second due in Dec 2013. The third film is expected in summer 2014, Warner Bros announces. \uc2 Time Warner\uc2  (TWX) down 1% to $38.61. (michael.calia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:36 EDT - Despite Supervalu's (SVU) continued jabber about lower prices, BMO expects new CEO Wayne Sales to focus more on differentiating SVU's chains from other grocers than becoming more-aggressive pricewise so as to avoid a war with \uc2 Kroger\uc2  (KR) and \uc2 Safeway\uc2  (SWY). Sales said in a letter to employees that SVU's goal is to be competitively priced, "and we will move as quickly as possible to achieve this." But differentiation was more his style when he orchestrated a turnaround of Canadian Tire (CTC.A.T) last decade. Meanwhile, BMO says SVU "has become so irrelevant with the consumer that recapturing permanent share will be impossible," and that "challenges may prove to be insurmountable at this stage for even the most-exceptional food retail executive." (annie.gasparro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:33 EDT - While analysts are positive on \uc2 Standard Pacific\uc2 's (SPF) strategy of targeting move-up buyers and raising prices, Williams Financial is jittery about building homes before there are buyers in place. "The company's large spec unit sales will likely temper gross margin expansion and [we] would prefer to see greater restraint in spec sales volumes in areas where land replenishment costs are high," firm writes. "The higher volumes through a spec strategy should be reserved to those regions with ample land opportunities and limited ability to drive prices." SPF up 2.7% at $6.18. (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 07-30-12 1328ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120730e87u000fr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
