\par\par \b \uc2 Japan's Azumi Issues Warning As Yen Hits Highs\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2012\par 05:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  finance minister threatens \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as yen hits fresh highs\par \par \uc2 --The minister says the yen's rise is having an impact on Japan exporters\par \par \uc2 --The prime minister and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor hold a closed-door meeting\par \par \uc2 --The BOJ governor renews his pledge to continue strong easing\par \par \uc2 TOKYO--Japan's finance minister on Monday sent out a fresh warning about the strong yen as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted new highs against the euro and the dollar, clouding the prospects for manufacturers and causing Tokyo stocks to drop.\par \par \uc2 Intensifying worries over Europe's debt crisis as Spain's fiscal state deteriorates drove the common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 94.87 yen in Asian trading, a level unseen since late 2000. The dollar also touched Y78.15, the lowest in a month and a half, and below average forecast of Japan's big manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's movement is having an impact on exporters and other parts" of the economy, Finance Minister Jun Azumi told reporters at the ministry. "My stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unchanged and will never change under any circumstance. As I've been saying, I will take decisive steps against speculative movement of excessive volatility. As far as the current situation is concerned, I'm watching it carefully."\par \par \uc2 Separately, Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda and Bank of Japan Gov. \uc2 Masaaki Shirakawa\uc2  held a meeting Monday, the first such meeting since April 6, according to Chief Cabinet Secretary Osamu Fujimura. Little is known about what the two discussed. Mr. Fujimura said only the meeting was aimed at reinforcing ties between the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But analysts speculate they may have explored ways to bolster growth and end Japan's chronic deflation, which theoretically strengthens the yen.\par \par \uc2 All these developments suggest that even though some \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  officials have spoken recently of the advantages of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --such as by making imports less expensive--the government is on high alert against further yen rises.\par \par \uc2 Rises in the yen make exports less competitive abroad. Japan's economy has recently shown signs of recovery as government spending has reinvigorated domestic demand, but analysts warn that a sharp slowdown in exports could derail the fragile rebound. That doesn't portend well for Mr. Noda, who needs solid growth to implement his key policy of sales tax hikes to mend Japan's tattered finances.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Azumi's phrase "decisive steps" is code for possible direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter the yen's gains. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last undertook \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early November after a series of verbal warnings.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Azumi's latest rhetoric appeared to have little impact on the yen, which marked the day's high after he spoke. Analysts say that even though manufacturers continue to plea for government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , more yen gains may be needed to spur Tokyo to act.\par \par \uc2 In a parliamentary session after his talk with Mr. Noda, Mr. Shirakawa repeated his pledge to maintain an ultra-easy monetary policy until the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 1% inflation goal is in sight. Mr. Shirakawa, however, provided no indications of fresh easing measures.\par \par \uc2 -Toko Sekiguchi, Megumi Fujikawa contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 07-22-12 2355ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020120723e87n0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
