\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,878 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juli 2012\par 01:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    78.10-78.82      77.98-79.17 \par EUR/USD   1.2000-1.2182    1.1876-1.2284 \par AUD/USD   1.0284-1.0392    1.0232-1.0428 \par NZD/USD   0.7919-0.7998    0.7884-0.8040 \par GBP/USD   1.5580-1.5673    1.5551-1.5735 \par USD/CHF   0.9855-0.9949    0.9773-1.0000 \par USD/CAD   1.0094-1.0149    1.0062-1.0173 \par EUR/JPY    94.00-95.63      93.00-96.70 \par EUR/GBP   0.7692-0.7792    0.7388-0.7820 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to trade with bearish bias after hitting near-seven-week low of 78.38 this morning. Undermined by capital flight to safe-haven JPY amid elevated risk aversion (VIX fear gauge rose 5.31% to 16.27, S&P fell 1.01% Friday) as fears mount that Spain could be forced into a full-fledged sovereign bailout, Greece may run out of money as early as September, unease over finances of the Italian region of Sicily. USD/JPY also weighed by Japan exporter sales; higher prospects of a third round of U.S. quantitative easing. But USD/JPY losses tempered by demand from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importers; wariness over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Data focus: 1230 GMT U.S. June Chicago Fed National Activity Index. USD/JPY daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, while stochastics stay suppressed at oversold, 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 78.10 (June 5 low); breach would target 77.98 (June 4 low), then 77.65 (June 1 low). Resistance at 78.80-78.82 (Friday's high-Thursday's high), then at 79.17 (Tuesday's high) and 79.40 (July 13 high); breach would expose upside to 79.97 (July 12 high), then 80.10 (July 5 high), 80.63 (June 25 high) and 80.91 (50% Fibonacci correction of decline from March 15 high of 84.18 to June 1 low of 77.65).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade with bearish bias after hitting fresh two-year low of 1.2103 this morning. Weighed by heightened fears over Spain's wilting economy and government finances after a downward revision to Spanish economic growth expectations and Spanish region of Valencia said it plans to seek aid from the government; Egan-Jones cut Spain's credit rating further into junk status; \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  saying it would reject Greek bonds as collateral for the time being; Der Spiegel report that the IMF has signaled to Brussels it will no longer partake in financial aid for Greece, meaning Athens could run out of money as early as September; jitters over debt contagion spreading to Italy as its 10-year bond yield climbed sharply above 6% and S&P suspended Sicily's rating Friday as concerns mount the Italian region may default; worries over broader euro-zone economic slowdown. Data focus: 1400 GMT euro-zone July FCCI flash consumer confidence indicator. EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish; stochastics reverting to bearish mode at oversold. Support at the psychological 1.2000 line; breach would expose downside to 1.1876 (June 7, 2010 low), then 1.1826 (Feb. 27, 2006 low) and 1.1640 (Nov. 15, 2005 low). Resistance at 1.2182 (hourly chart), then at 1.2284 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.2325-1.2334 band (Thursday's high-July 9 high), then 1.2401 (July 6 high) and 1.2538 (July 5 high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by intensified investor risk aversion. But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest-rate differential; Aussie demand on buoyant AUD/NZD cross; sovereign demand for Aussie. Data focus: 0130 GMT Australia 2Q PPI. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, 5- & 15-day moving averages rising; but stochastics have turned bearish at overbought. Support at 1.0284 (Wednesday's low), then at 1.0232 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0197 (July 16 low), then 1.0118 (July 13 low), 1.0096 (July 12 low) and 1.0014 (June 29 low). Resistance at 1.0392 (hourly chart), then at 1.0428 (Friday's high) and 1.0445 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0474 (April 27 reaction high), then 1.0557 (March 27 high).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by elevated investor risk aversion; Kiwi sales on buoyant AUD/NZD cross. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics have turned bearish. Support at 0.7919 (Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7884 (July 13 low), then 0.7857 (July 12 low) and 0.7844-0.7835 band (June 29 low-June 28 low). Resistance at 0.7998 (hourly chart), then at 0.8040 (Friday's high) and 0.8054 (Thursday's high); breach would target 0.8074-0.8081 band (July 5 reaction high-61.8% Fibonacci retracement of decline from Feb. 29 high of 0.8470 to June 1 low of 0.7451), then 0.8234 (April 30 high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by strong investor risk aversion; higher-than-expected U.K. June public sector net borrowing of GBP14.4 billion (vs GBP13.3 billion forecast). But GBP/USD losses tempered by GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turning bearish at overbought. Support at 1.5580 (Wednesday's low), then at 1.5551 (Tuesday's low) and 1.5516 (July 16 low); breach would expose downside to 1.5410 (July 13 low), then 1.5390 (July 12 low), 1.5320 (June 5 low) and 1.5265 (June 1 swing low). Resistance 1.5673 (hourly chart), then at 1.5725-1.5735 band (Friday's high-Thursday's high), 1.5748 (200-day moving average) and 1.5777 (June 20 reaction high, near 50% Fibonacci retracement of decline from April 30 high of 1.6301 to June 1 low of 1.5265); breach would expose upside to 1.5848 (May 22 high), then 1.5905 (61.8% retracement) and psychological 1.6000 line.\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with bullish bias after hitting fresh 19-month high of 0.9935 this morning. Underpinned by broadly stronger demand for safe-haven USD as risk aversion mounts; \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as EUR/CHF hovers just above 1.2000 floor (cross last at 1.2008). But USD/CHF gains tempered by CHF demand on EUR/CHF cross. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD bullish; stochastics reverting to bullish mode at overbought. Resistance at 0.9949 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of 1.1730-0.7068 June 1, 2010-Aug. 9, 2011 decline); breach would expose upside to the psychological 1.0000 line, then 1.0066 (Dec. 1, 2010 reaction high) and 1.0277 (Sept. 10, 2010 high). Support at 0.9855 (hourly chart), then at 0.9773 (Friday's low) and 0.9743-0.9733 band (Thursday's low-July 10 low); breach would expose downside to 0.9680 (July 6 low), then 0.9576 (July 5 low, near 55-day moving average), 0.9509 (July 3 low) and 0.9475 (July 2 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade with bullish bias. Underpinned by inflamed investor risk aversion; weaker oil prices (Nymex crude settled down $1.22 Friday at $91.44/bbl); lower-than-expected Canada June CPI (headline CPI +1.5% vs +1.7% forecast; core CPI +2.0% vs +2.3% forecast). USD/CAD daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics have turned bullish at oversold. Resistance at 1.0149 (Wednesday's high), then at 1.0169-1.0173 (Tuesday's high-July 16 high); breach would expose upside to 1.0203 (July 13 high), then 1.0249 (July 12 high), 1.0341 (June 29 high) and 1.0363 (June 28 high). Support at 1.0094 (hourly chart), then at 1.0066-1.0062 (Friday's low-Thursday's low), 1.0053 (base of descending channel formed with June 4 high of 1.0446 and June 20 low of 1.0155) and 1.0044 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of 0.9796-1.0446 April 27-June 4 advance); breach would expose downside to 0.9986 (May 15 low), then 0.9949 (May 11 low) and 0.9920 (May 8 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to trade with bearish bias after hitting fresh 11-year low of 94.88 this morning. Undermined by heightened investor risk aversion as worries mount over the euro-zone debt crisis; Japan exporter sales. But EUR/JPY losses tempered by demand from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importers; wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; five- & 15-day moving averages falling. There's no significant support, except for psychological round-numbered levels of 94.00 down to 92.00, until 91.35 (November 2000 low). Resistance at 95.63 (hourly chart), then at 96.70 (Friday's high) and 96.83 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 97.32-97.38 band (Wednesday's high-Tuesday's high), then 97.90 (July 12 high) and 98.05-98.13 band (July 10 high-July 9 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting 45-month low of 0.7753 this morning. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, five- & 15-day moving averages falling, while stochastics stay suppressed at oversold. Support at 0.7692 (Oct. 20, 2008 reaction low); breach would expose downside to 0.7388 (Jan. 28, 2008 low). Resistance at 0.7792 (hourly chart), then at 0.7820 (Friday's high), 0.7853 (Thursday's high) and 0.7868-0.7877 band (Wednesday's high-July 16 high); breach would target 0.7913 (July 13 high), 0.7934 (July 10 high), 0.7948 (July 9 high) and 0.7983 (July 6 high).\par \par \uc2 Write to Jerry Tan at jerry.tan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security.\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 22-07-12 2331GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1075 4033   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120722e87m00026\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
