orries over global growth heightened after weak U.S. retail sales data, Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao warned that a recovery in the country's economic growth isn't yet stable, and the IMF cut its global growth outlook,. But USD/JPY losses tempered by demand from Japan importers and investment trusts; wariness of stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Data focus: 1230 GMT U.S. June CPI, 1300 GMT U.S. May Treasury international capital flows, 1315 GMT U.S. June industrial production & capacity utilization, 1400 GMT U.S. July NAHB housing market index, 1715 GMT Fed's Pianalto speaks. USD/JPY daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 78.68-78.61 band (Monday's low-June 15 low); breach would expose downside to 78.10 (June 5 low), then 77.98 (June 4 low) and 77.65 (June 1 low). Resistance at 79.15 (hourly chart), then at 79.40 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 79.97 (Thursday's high), then 80.10 (July 5 high), 80.63 (June 25 high) and 80.91 (50% Fibonacci correction of decline from March 15 high of 84.18 to June 1 low of 77.65).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade in higher range. Underpinned by negative USD sentiment. But EUR/USD gains tempered by diminished risk appetite; persistent worries over ongoing euro-zone debt crisis amid reports that the ECB supported imposing losses on Spanish bondholders, while German Constitutional Court announced that it would not decide on the constitutionality of the European bailout fund until Sept. 12; worries over euro-zone economic slowdown; caution before Fed Chairman Bernanke's Monetary Policy Report to U.S. Senate Banking Committee. Data focus: 0900 GMT Germany July ZEW indicator of economic sentiment. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics bullish at oversold. Resistance at 1.2290-1.2297 band (Monday's high-Wednesday's high), then at 1.2334 (July 9 high); breach would expose upside to 1.2401 (July 6 high), then 1.2538 (July 5 high), 1.2608 (July 4 high) and 1.2627 (July 3 high). Support at 1.2212 (hourly chart), then at 1.2175 (Monday's low), 1.2162 (Friday's low) and 1.2151 (June 29, 2010 low); breach would expose downside to the psychological 1.2000 line, then 1.1876 (June 7, 2010 low), 1.1826 (Feb. 27, 2006 low) and 1.1640 (Nov. 15, 2005 low).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to trade with risks skewed higher. Spotlight on 0130 GMT Australia July RBA monetary policy meeting minutes. AUD/USD underpinned by negative USD sentiment; firmer commodity prices (CRB spot index closed up 2.02 Monday at 295.98); AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie. But AUD/USD gains tempered by subdued risk appetite; worries over China's economic growth slowdown; increased expectations of rate cuts from RBA in coming months; caution ahead of Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate. Data focus: 0130 GMT Australia June new motor vehicles sales. Daily chart mixed as MACD in bearish mode, but stochastics neutral. Resistance at 1.0259 (Monday's high, matching Thursday's high), then at 1.0281 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0328 (July 5 reaction high), then 1.0368 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of 1.0856-0.9579 Feb. 29-June 1 decline) and 1.0474 (April 27 reaction high). Support at 1.0197 (Monday's low), then at 1.0118 (Friday's low) and 1.0096 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0014 (June 29 low), then 0.9992 (June 28 low) and 0.9965 (June 25 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by negative USD sentiment; firmer commodity prices; NZD-USD yield gap. But NZD/USD gains tempered by lower-than-expected NZ 2Q CPI of +0.3% (vs +0.5% forecast); receding risk appetite; caution ahead of Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics neutral; 5- & 15-day moving averages meandering sideways. Resistance at 0.7987 (Monday's high), then at 0.7998 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8047 (July 6 high), then 0.8074-0.8081 band (July 5 reaction high-61.8% Fibonacci retracement of decline from Feb. 29 high of 0.8470 to June 1 low of 0.7451) and 0.8234 (April 30 high). Support at 0.7927 (Monday's low), then at 0.7884 (Friday's low); breach would target 0.7857 (Thursday's low), then 0.7844-0.7835 band (June 29 low-June 28 low), 0.7808 (June 15 low) and 0.7745 (June 14 low).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by negative USD sentiment; GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. But GBP/USD gains tempered by weak risk appetite; caution ahead of Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate. Data focus: 0830 GMT U.K. June inflation. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD in bullish mode; stochastics rising from oversold; bullish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 1.5610 Monday. Resistance at 1.5654 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.5693 (55-day moving average), then 1.5721 (July 2 reaction high), 1.5732 (June 21 high), 1.5750 (200-day moving average), 1.5777 (June 20 high, near 50% Fibonacci retracement of decline from April 30 high of 1.6301 to June 1 low of 1.5265) and 1.5848 (May 22 high). Support at 1.5569 (hourly chart), then at 1.5516 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.5410 (Friday's low), then 1.5390 (Thursday's low), 1.5320 (June 5 low) and 1.5265 (June 1 swing low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade in lower range. Undermined by negative USD sentiment; CHF demand on EUR/CHF cross. But USD/CHF losses tempered by \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as EUR/CHF hovers just above 1.2000 floor (cross last at 1.2010). Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics bearish at overbought. Support at 0.9768 (Monday's low, matching Wednesday's low), then at 0.9733 (July 10 low); breach would expose downside to 0.9680 (July 6 low), then 0.9576 (July 5 low), 0.9539 (55-day moving average), 0.9509 (July 3 low) and 0.9475 (July 2 low). Resistance at 0.9834 (hourly chart), then at 0.9864-0.9872 band (Monday's high-Friday's 19-month high); breach would expose upside to 0.9949 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of 1.1730-0.7068 June 1, 2010-Aug. 9, 2011 decline), then the psychological 1.0000 line, 1.0066 (Dec. 1, 2010 reaction high) and 1.0277 (Sept. 10, 2010 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to consolidate with risks skewed lower as markets await 1300 GMT \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  interest rate announcement: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to maintain its policy rate at 1.00%. USD/CAD undermined by negative USD sentiment; stronger commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled up $1.33 Monday at $88.43/bbl). But USD/CAD losses tempered by low investor risk appetite; caution ahead of Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate. Other data focus: 1230 GMT Canada May survey of manufacturing. USD/CAD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode; though inside-day-range pattern completed Monday. Support at 1.0127-1.0125 (Monday's low-Friday low); breach would target 1.0106 (200-day moving average), then 1.0096 (July 5 low), 1.0070 (100-day moving average), 1.0044 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of 0.9796-1.0446 April 27-June 4 advance), 0.9986 (May 15 low) and 0.9949 (May 11 reaction low). Resistance at 1.0173 (Monday's high), then at 1.0203 (Friday's high) and 1.0249 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0341 (June 29 high), then 1.0363 (June 28 high), 1.0446 (June 4 high) and 1.0523 (Nov. 25 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to consolidate after hitting six-week low of 96.17 Monday. Underpinned by demand from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importers; wariness of stealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But EUR/JPY topside limited by diminished risk appetite; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  exporter sales; caution ahead of Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate. Daily chart still negative-biased as MACD bearish, 5- & 15-day moving averages falling, while stochastics stay suppressed at oversold. Support at 96.17 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 95.59 (11-year low hit on June 1), then psychological round-numbered levels of 95.00 down to 92.00, then 91.35 (November 2000 low). Resistance at 97.33 (Monday's high), then at 97.90 (Thursday's high) and 98.05-98.13 band (July 10 high-July 9 high); breach would expose upside to 99.10 (July 6 high), then 100.09 (55-day moving average), 100.33 (July 5 high), 100.65 (July 4 high) and 100.84 (July 3 high).\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires}}}\uc2  [ 16-07-12 2330GMT ]\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting fresh 44-month low of 0.7829 Monday. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, 5- & 15-day moving averages falling, while stochastics stay suppressed at oversold. Support at 0.7829 (Monday's low); breach would target 0.7807 (Oct. 31, 2008 low), then 0.7692 (Oct. 20, 2008 reaction low). Resistance at 0.7877 (Monday's high), then at 0.7913 (Friday's high), 0.7934 (July 10 high) and 0.7948 (July 9 high); breach would expose upside to 0.7983 (July 6 high), then 0.8041 (July 5 high), 0.8053 (July 4 high) and 0.8077 (July 2 high).\par \par \uc2 Write to Jerry Tan at jerry.tan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors wh