\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2012\par 02:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The euro rose off of a two-year low against the dollar, snapping a three-day losing streak, while the Australian dollar rallied as Chinese growth met expectations. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended the New York trading day at $1.2250, up from $1.2203 late Thursday. The pound was recently at $1.5570, up from $1.5428 late Thursday. Against the yen, the dollar fetched Y79.20 from Y79.32 late Thursday. The Australian dollar recently traded at $1.0225 from $1.0138 late Thursday. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recently traded at 83.297, down from 83.659 late Thursday.Investors are paying closer attention to Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's testimony before the Senate Tuesday for clues of additional Fed action.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate in higher range after hitting fresh two-year low of 1.2162 Friday. Underpinned by improved investor risk tolerance; weaker USD sentiment; successful Italian EUR3.5 billion auction of three-year government bonds despite \uc2 Moody's\uc2  two-notch sovereign downgrade of the country. But EUR/USD gains tempered by concerns over Spain's fiscal and economic health; worries over broader euro-zone economic slowdown; caution before Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate Tuesday. Data focus: 0900 GMT euro-zone May trade balance, June harmonized CPI. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, 5- & 15-day moving averages falling; but stochastics turned bullish at oversold; bullish outside-day-range pattern completed Friday. Resistance at 1.2274 (this morning's high), then at 1.2297 (Wednesday's high) and 1.2334 (July 9 high); breach would expose upside to 1.2401 (July 6 high), then 1.2538 (July 5 high), 1.2608 (July 4 high) and 1.2627 (July 3 high). Support at 1.2203 (hourly chart), then at 1.2162 (Friday's low) and 1.2151 (June 29, 2010 low); breach would expose downside to the psychological 1.2000 line, then 1.1876 (June 7, 2010 low), 1.1826 (Feb. 27, 2006 low) and 1.1640 (Nov. 15, 2005 low).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting three-week low of 79.07 Friday. Liquidity thin in Asia as Japan markets closed today for holiday. USD/JPY undermined by negative USD sentiment (ICE spot dollar index last 83.27 vs 83.61 early Friday) after surprise drop in University of Michigan's consumer sentiment index to 72.0 at start of July from 73.2 in June (vs forecast for rise to 73.5). USD/JPY also weighed by buy-yen orders from Japan exporters. But USD/JPY losses tempered by sell-yen orders from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importers; wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's stealth yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  even as Tokyo markets closed; yen-funded carry trades amid stronger investor risk tolerance (VIX fear gauge eased 8.67% to 16.74) after China 2Q GDP growth of +7.6% was not as bad as some had feared, while Italy successfully sold EUR3.5 billion of three-year government bonds despite two-notch sovereign downgrade by \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , and U.S. stocks surged Friday (S&P up 1.65%) on better-than-expected quarterly results from \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 . USD/JPY downside also limited by positions adjustment ahead of Federal Reserve Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate Tuesday. Data focus: 1230 GMT U.S. June retail sales, July Empire State manufacturing survey, 1400 GMT U.S. May business inventories. USD/JPY daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, 5- & 15-day moving averages falling, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold. Support at 79.07 (Friday's low); breach would target 78.79 (June 20 low), then 78.65-78.61 (June 18 low-June 15 low) and 78.10 (June 5 low). Resistance at 79.40 (Friday's high), then at 79.97 (Thursday's high) and 80.10 (July 5 high); breach would expose upside to 80.63 (June 25 high), then 80.91 (50% Fibonacci correction of decline from March 15 high of 84.18 to June 1 low of 77.65), 81.68 (61.8% correction level) and 81.78 (April 20 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD to consolidate in higher range after hitting seven-day high of 1.5591 this morning. Underpinned by increased investor risk tolerance; weaker USD sentiment; GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. But GBP/USD gains tempered by caution before Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate Tuesday. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD turning bullish; stochastics rising from oversold. Resistance at 1.5623 (July 5 high); breach would expose upside to 1.5703 (55-day moving average), then 1.5721 (July 2 reaction high), 1.5732 (June 21 high) and 1.5750 (200-day moving average). Support at 1.5455 (hourly chart), then at 1.5410 (Friday's low) and 1.5390 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.5320 (June 5 low), then 1.5265 (June 1 swing low), 1.5233 (Jan. 13 swing low) and the psychological 1.5000 line.\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting 19-month high of 0.9872 Friday. Undermined by weaker USD sentiment; broadly softer demand for safe-haven USD as risk tolerance improved; CHF demand on EUR/CHF cross. But USD/CHF losses tempered by \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as EUR/CHF hovers just above 1.2000 floor (cross last at 1.2008). Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, 5- & 15-day moving averages rising; but stochastics turned bearish at overbought; bearish outside-day-range pattern completed Friday. Support at 0.9767 (Wednesday's low), then at 0.9733 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9680 (July 6 low), then 0.9576 (July 5 low), 0.9526 (55-day moving average), 0.9509 (July 3 low) and 0.9475 (July 2 low). Resistance at 0.9839 (hourly chart), then at 0.9872 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to the psychological 1.0000 line, then 1.0066 (Dec. 1, 2010 reaction high) and 1.0277 (Sept. 10, 2010 high).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD to consolidate in lower range after hitting one-week low of 1.0125 Friday. Undermined by weaker USD sentiment; improved investor risk tolerance; stronger commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled up $1.02 Friday at $87.10/bbl). But USD/CAD losses tempered by caution before Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate Tuesday. Data focus: 1230 GMT Canada May international transactions in securities. USD/CAD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode. Support at 1.0125 (Friday low); breach would target 1.0107 (200-day moving average), then 1.0096 (July 5 low), 1.0068 (100-day moving average), 0.9986 (May 15 low) and 0.9949 (May 11 reaction low). Resistance at 1.0184 (hourly chart), then a 1.0203 (Friday's high) and 1.0249 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0341 (June 29 high), then 1.0363 (June 28 high), 1.0446 (June 4 high) and 1.0523 (Nov. 25 high).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD to range-trade. Underpinned by stronger investor risk tolerance; weaker USD sentiment; firmer commodity prices; AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie. But AUD/USD gains tempered by increased expectations of rate cuts from RBA in coming months; caution before Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate Tuesday. Data focus: 0130 GMT Australia May lending finance. Daily chart mixed as MACD in bearish mode, but stochastics neutral; 5-day moving average meandering sideways above rising 15-day MA. Resistance at 1.0259 (Thursday's high), then at 1.0281 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0328 (July 5 reaction high), then 1.0368 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of 1.0856-0.9579 Feb. 29-June 1 decline) and 1.0474 (April 27 reaction high). Support at 1.0159 (hourly chart), then at 1.0118 (Friday's low) and 1.0096 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0014 (June 29 low), then 0.9992 (June 28 low) and 0.9965 (June 25 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD to range-trade. Underpinned by higher investor risk tolerance; weaker USD sentiment; firmer commodity prices; NZD-USD yield gap. But NZD/USD gains tempered by caution before Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony before U.S. Senate Tuesday. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turning neutral. Resistance at 0.7998 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8047 (July 6 high), then 0.8074-0.8081 band (July 5 reaction high-61.8% Fibonacci retracement of decline from Feb. 29 high of 0.8470 to June 1 low of 0.7451) and 0.8234 (April 30 high). Support at 0.7904 (hourly chart), then at 0.7884 (Friday's low); breach would target 0.7857 (Thursday's low), then 0.7844-0.7835 band (June 29 low-June 28 low), 0.7808 (June 15 low) and 0.7745 (June 14 low).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan fell against the U.S. dollar late Friday after the release of data showing economic growth slowed further in the second quarter and as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sought to guide the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower through its daily fixing. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3789 around 0830 GMT, higher than Thursday's close of CNY6.3733. It traded in a range of CNY6.3751 to CNY6.3818. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3247, higher than Thursday's 6.3215. Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts rose to 6.4230/6.4280 from 6.4180/6.4280 late Thursday, implying a 0.7% fall by the yuan over the next year. In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar was at CNY6.3760 late Friday, higher than CNY6.3695 late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120716e87g0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
