\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2012\par 02:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The euro slumped to another two-year low against the dollar Thursday, slipping below $1.22, as investors worried that major central banks were holding their fire as the global economy cools. The euro touched a new two-year low of $1.2166 earlier and ended the New York trading day at 1.2203, compared with $1.2240 late Wednesday. This was the first time the euro fell below $1.22 since July 2010, according to EBS via CQG. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been hitting a series of two-year lows in the past week as European leaders have struggled to make progress in addressing the region's debt crisis. Meanwhile, the yen benefited from safe-haven flows, further supported by the outcome of the Bank of Japan's policy meeting, where central bankers decided against another round of easing. The dollar was at Y79.31, down from Y79.743 late Wednesday. The euro recently fetched Y96.80 from Y97.62, falling earlier to a six-week low of Y96.43. The Australian dollar recently changed hands at $1.0138, down 1% from $1.0252 late Wednesday. The ICE Dollar Index was recently at 83.636 from 83.568 late Wednesday. The pound was recently trading at $1.5431, down from $1.5503.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting fresh two-year low of 1.2166 Thursday. Undermined by positive USD sentiment; global economic growth concerns; rise in Spanish and Italian 10-year yields Thursday as worries over ongoing euro-zone debt crisis persist. But EUR/USD losses tempered by positions adjustment as short-term speculators trim exposure before weekend. EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 1.2166 (Thursday's low), then at 1.2151 (June 29, 2010 low); breach would expose downside to the psychological 1.2000 line, then 1.1876 (June 7, 2010 low), 1.1826 (Feb. 27, 2006 low) and 1.1640 (Nov. 15, 2005 low). Resistance at 1.2248 (Thursday's high), then at 1.2297 (Wednesday's high) and 1.2334 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.2401 (July 6 high), then 1.2538 (July 5 high), 1.2608 (July 4 high) and 1.2627 (July 3 high).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to trade in lower range. Spotlight on 0200 GMT China 2Q GDP data. USD/JPY undermined by flows to safe-haven JPY and unwinding of JPY-funded carry trades amid increased risk aversion as anxiety rose over global economic growth after South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly lowered rates alongside Brazil's rate cut to record lows to support their economies, Australia's unemployment rate rose in June, while the euro-zone debt crisis festers and Wednesday's FOMC minutes showed U.S. Federal Reserve unwilling to adopt more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance to stimulate growth. USD/JPY also weighed by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  exporter sales, lower U.S. Treasury yields. But USD/JPY losses tempered by demand from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importers; wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; positive USD sentiment after bigger-than-expected 26,000 fall in latest U.S. weekly jobless claims to more-than-four-year low of 350,000; positions adjustment before Japan's long weekend (Japan's markets closed for holiday on Monday). USD/JPY daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode. Support at 79.13-79.08 band (Tuesday's low-June 29 low); breach would target 78.79 (June 20 low), then 78.65-78.61 (June 18 low-June 15 low) and 78.10 (June 5 low). Resistance at 79.97 (Thursday's high), then at 80.10 (July 5 high); breach would expose upside to 80.63 (June 25 high), then 80.91 (50% Fibonacci correction of decline from March 15 high of 84.18 to June 1 low of 77.65), 81.68 (61.8% correction level) and 81.78 (April 20 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting five-week low of 1.5390 Thursday. Undermined by positive USD sentiment; intensified investor risk aversion; GBP sales on rebounding EUR/GBP cross. But GBP/USD losses tempered by positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, while stochastics stay suppressed at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 1.5390 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.5320 (June 5 low), then 1.5265 (June 1 swing low), 1.5233 (Jan. 13 swing low) and the psychological 1.5000 line. Resistance at 1.5477 (hourly chart), then at 1.5515 (Thursday's high) and 1.5578 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.5623 (July 5 high), then 1.5721 (July 2 reaction high, near 55-day moving average), 1.5732 (June 21 high) and 1.5749 (200-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate with bullish bias after hitting 19-month high of 0.9870 on EBS Thursday. Underpinned by positive USD sentiment; broadly stronger demand for safe-haven USD amid mounting investor risk aversion; \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as EUR/CHF hovers just above 1.2000 floor. But USD/CHF gains tempered by CHF demand on EUR/CHF cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Data focus: 0715 GMT Swiss June import price index & PPI. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; 5- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at 0.9870 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to the psychological 1.0000 line, then 1.0066 (Dec. 1, 2010 reaction high) and 1.0277 (Sept. 10, 2010 high). Support at 0.9800 (Thursday's low), then at 0.9767 (Wednesday's low) and 0.9733 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9680 (July 6 low), then 0.9576 (July 5 low), 0.9509 (July 3 low, near 55-day moving average) and 0.9475 (July 2 low).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to range-trade. Underpinned by positive USD sentiment; increased investor risk aversion. But USD/CAD topside limited by buoyant oil prices; positions adjustment before weekend. USD/CAD daily chart mixed as MACD in bearish mode, stochastics turning bearish, but bullish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 1.0230 Thursday. Support at 1.0173-1.0163 band (Thursday's low-Tuesday's low), then at 1.0137 (July 6 low); breach would target 1.0108 (200-day moving average), then 1.0096 (July 5 low), 1.0067 (100-day moving average), 0.9986 (May 15 low) and 0.9949 (May 11 reaction low). Resistance at 1.0249 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0341 (June 29 high), then 1.0363 (June 28 high), 1.0446 (June 4 high) and 1.0523 (Nov. 25 high).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting two-week low of 1.0096 Thursday. Undermined by increased expectations of further rate cuts from RBA after weaker-than-expected Australian June jobs data; positive USD sentiment, heightened investor risk aversion; softer commodity prices (CRB spot index closed down 0.52 Thursday at 290.27); Aussie sales on retreating AUD/NZD cross. But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie; positions adjustment before weekend. Aussie vulnerable to 0200 GMT China 2Q GDP data. Daily negative-biased as MACD & stochastics turned bearish; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 1.0150 Thursday. Support at 1.0096 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0014 (June 29 low), then 0.9992 (June 28 low) and 0.9965 (June 25 reaction low). Resistance at 1.0210 (hourly chart), then at 1.0259 (Thursday's high) and 1.0281 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0328 (July 5 reaction high), then 1.0368 (61.8% Fibonacci retracement of 1.0856-0.9579 Feb. 29-June 1 decline) and 1.0474 (April 27 reaction high).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting two-week low of 0.7857 Thursday. Undermined by positive USD sentiment; inflamed investor risk aversion; softer commodity prices. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap; Kiwi demand on retreating AUD/NZD cross; positions adjustment before weekend. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish; 5-day moving average staged bearish crossover against 15-day MA; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.7925 Thursday. Support at 0.7857 (Thursday's low), then at 0.7844-0.7835 band (June 29 low-June 28 low); breach would expose downside to 0.7808 (June 15 low), then 0.7745 (June 14 low) and 0.7712 (June 13 low). Resistance at 0.7945 (hourly chart), the at 0.7978 (Thursday's high) and 0.7998 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8047 (July 6 high), then 0.8074-0.8081 band (July 5 reaction high-61.8% Fibonacci retracement of decline from Feb. 29 high of 0.8470 to June 1 low of 0.7451) and 0.8234 (April 30 high).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan fell against the U.S. dollar late Thursday with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guiding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, after the greenback rose overnight following minutes from the latest U.S. Federal Reserve policy meeting showed officials were split over the timing and necessity of further stimulus. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3733 around 0830 GMT, up from CNY6.3686 late Wednesday. It traded in a range of CNY6.3682 to CNY6.3746. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3215, slightly higher than Wednesday's 6.3209. Investors are not sure whether China's economic growth could rebound in the third quarter given the ongoing European debt crisis, so they're more willing to hold the dollar.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120713e87d000dy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
