\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 2012\par 02:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The euro gained Thursday on a report that global central banks are preparing for a coordinated move to provide liquidity to the market if needed after this weekend's Greek elections. Late Thursday, the euro was at $1.2633 against the dollar versus $1.2556 late in New York Wednesday. The dollar was last at Y79.35 from Y79.48.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade in higher range. Buoyed by weaker USD sentiment; report that major central banks plan to take coordinated action if needed to ensure global financial markets stay well-funded after Sunday's Greek elections; rumor of "secret" Greek opinion polls favoring pro-bailout New Democracy party in Sunday's parliamentary elections. But EUR/USD gains tempered by worries over Spain as government bond yields rose above critical 7% level for first time in euro era; warning from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that mounting uncertainty about the future of the euro threatens the region's prosperity for years to come; fears of contagion to Italy - Rome sold EUR4.5 billion of bonds Thurday, the maximum it had targeted, but at much higher rates than in previous auctions; ratings firm Egan-Jones's downgrade of France to triple-B-plus from A-minus - three notches away from junk status; positions adjustment before weekend and Greek elections - some still fear could result in Greece leaving the euro. Data focus: 0900 GMT euro-zone April trade balance, euro-zone 1Q employment. EUR/USD daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode. Resistance at 1.2637 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.2672 (Monday's high), then 1.2825 (May 21 high), 1.2869 (May 15 high) and 1.2879 (55-day moving average). Support at 1.2586 (hourly chart), then at 1.2542 (Thursday's low) and 1.2472 (Wednesday's low); breach would target 1.2442-1.2435 band (Tuesday's low-June 8 low), then 1.2410 (June 5 low), 1.2375 (June 4 low) and 1.2288 (June 1 23-month low).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate in higher range. Spotlight on BOJ monetary policy decision: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to maintain the size of its asset-purchase program at Y70 trillion and its policy rate in a 0.0%-0.1% range. USD/JPY undermined by weaker USD sentiment after surprise rise in latest U.S. weekly jobless claims to 386,000 (vs 375,000 forecast) from previous week's upwardly revised 380,000 (original estimate 377,000), in-line 0.3% drop in U.S. consumer prices in May for first decline in two years, wider-than-expected U.S. 1Q current account deficit of $137.3 billion (vs $134.0 bln forecast); speculation that U.S. Federal Reserve might implement another round of quantitative easing at Wednesday's meeting; Japan exporter sales. But USD/JPY supported by demand from Japan importers (demand likely higher at today's Tokyo fixing); expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  vice finance minister for international affairs Nakao told The Wall Street Journal Thursday the yen's rise has been "one-sided" and warned his government would act if the yen continues to rise; reduced safe-haven appeal of yen and yen-funded carry trades as risk sentiment improves on increased expectations of further U.S. stimulus measures; report that major central banks are preparing coordinated efforts to provide liquidity if necessary after the Greek elections; chatter of "secret" opinion polls suggesting Greece's New Democracy party will win in elections this weekend and a pro-bailout Greek government will be formed; positions adjustment before weekend and Greek elections. Data focus: 1230 GMT U.S. June Empire State manufacturing survey, 1300 GMT U.S. April Treasury international capital data, 1315 GMT U.S. May industrial production & capacity utilization, 1355 GMT U.S. June University of Michigan survey of consumers (preliminary). USD/JPY daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought. Resistance at 79.50 (Thursday's high), then at 79.74 (Wednesday's high), 79.92 (Monday's high) and 80.08-80.14 band (May 23 high-May 22 high); breach would target 80.22 (confluence of 55-day & 100-day moving averages), then 80.39 (May 17 high), 80.56-80.61 band (May 16 high-May 2 high), 81.45 (April 27 high) and 81.71 (April 25 high). Support at 79.15-79.11 band (Thursday's low-June 8 low); breach would expose downside to 78.61 (June 6 low, near 200-day moving average), then 78.10 (June 5 low), 77.98 (June 4 low) and 77.65 (June 1 3.5-month low).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade in higher range. Underpinned by improved risk appetite; weaker USD sentiment; U.K. offering its banks unprecedented access to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds - in scheme dubbed "funding for lending" - if they vow to keep lending to British households and businesses. But GBP/USD gains tempered by GBP sales on rebounding EUR/GBP cross; worries over Spain and Italy; positions adjustment before weekend and Greek elections. Data focus: 0830 GMT U.K. April global goods trade balance. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; 5-day moving average has staged bullish crossover against 15-day MA. Resistance at 1.5563 (Thursday's high), then at 1.5597-1.5600 (Wednesday's high-June 7 reaction high); breach would expose upside to 1.5717 (May 28 high), then 1.5750 (200-day moving average) and 1.5838-1.5848 band (100-day moving average - May 22 high). Support at 1.5514 (hourly chart), then at 1.5470 (Thursday's low), 1.5453 (Tuesday's low) and 1.5401 (June 8 low); breach would target 1.5370 (June 6 low), then 1.5320 (June 5 low), 1.5265 (June 1 4.5-month low) and 1.5233 (Jan. 12 swing low).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade in lower range. Undermined by broadly softer demand for safe-haven USD as risk appetite improves; weaker USD sentiment; CHF demand on soft EUR/CHF cross as euro-zone worries persist. But USD/CHF losses tempered by \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as EUR/CHF hovers just above 1.2000 floor (cross last at 1.2009) - SNB Thursday left its key interest rate unchanged but reaffirmed its readiness to buy unlimited amounts of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the CHF1.20 floor against the euro; positions adjustment before weekend and Greek elections. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode; 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 0.9499 (Thursday's low), then at 0.9477 (Monday's low) and 0.9462 (May 23 low); breach would expose downside to 0.9363 (May 21 reaction low), then 0.9331 (55-day moving average) and 0.9238 (100-day moving average). Resistance at 0.9541 (hourly chart), then at 0.9574 (Thursday's high) and 0.9627 (Wednesday's high); breach would target 0.9650-0.9657 band (Tuesday's high-June 8 high), then 0.9677 (June 5 high), 0.9695 (June 4 high) and 0.9771-0.9785 band (June 1 15-month high-Jan. 11, 2011 high).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade in lower range. Undermined by improved risk appetite; weaker USD sentiment; firmer commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled up $1.29 Thursday at $83.91/bbl). But USD/CAD losses tempered by worries over Spain and Italy; positions adjustment before weekend and Greek elections. Data focus: 1230 GMT Canada April monthly survey of manufacturing. USD/CAD chart daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode; 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 1.0218 (Thursday's low), then at 1.0198 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0149 (May 22 low), then 1.0106 (200-day moving average) and 1.0076 (55-day moving average). Resistance at 1.0267 (hourly chart), then at 1.0295-1.0303 band (Thursday's high-Wednesday's high), 1.0325 (Tuesday's high) and 1.0354 (June 8 high); breach would target 1.0383 (June 6 high), then 1.0425 (June 5 high) and 1.0446 (June 4 six-month high).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to trade in higher range. Underpinned by better risk sentiment; firmer commodity prices (CRB spot index closed up 2.76 Thursday at 272.05); weaker USD sentiment; AUD-USD interest-rate differential; sovereign demand for Aussie. But AUD/USD gains tempered by worries over Spain and Italy; positions adjustment before weekend and Greek elections. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; bullish outside-day-range pattern completed yesterday; 5- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at 1.0033 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.0094 (55-day moving average), then 1.0219 (May 7 high). Support at 0.9961 (hourly chart), then at 0.9918 (Thursday's low) and 0.9846 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9817 (June 8 low), then 0.9733 (June 6 low), 0.9706 (June 5 low) and 0.9623 (June 4 low).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to trade in higher range. Underpinned by improved risk sentiment; firmer commodity prices; NZD-USD yield gap; weaker USD sentiment. But NZD/USD gains tempered by worries over Spain and Italy; positions adjustment before weekend and Greek elections. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought; 5- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at 0.7833 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.7895-0.7902 band (55-day moving average - May 10 high), then 0.7953 (200-day moving average), 0.7973 (May 8 high) and 0.8054 previous base set March 22. Support at 0.7785 (hourly chart), then at 0.7745 (Thursday's low) and 0.7712 (Wednesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7666 (Tuesday's low), then 0.7614 (June 8 low), 0.7555 (June 6 low) and 0.7516 (June 5 low).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan fell against the U.S. dollar late Thursday, as lingering concerns over the outlook for the euro zone ahead of weekend elections in Greece prompted investors to continue to seek safe-haven assets. On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3703 around 0830 GMT, higher than late Wednesday's CNY6.3691. It traded in a range of CNY6.3600 to CNY6.3711. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3191, lower than Wednesday's 6.3271. Since the start of June that the dollar/yuan is trading well above the central parity rate, showing investors' disapproval of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's decision.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120615e86f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
