\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Countries across world gird for Greece turmoil\b0\par\par\uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2012\par 13:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Plans being drawn up in case Greek election leads to market turmoil\par \par \uc2 * Swiss not ruling out capital controls\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , monetary easing most likely tools, sources say\par \par \uc2 * Tokyo won't set capital controls, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg\par \par \uc2 * China and India working on contingency plans\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Emma Thomasson\par \par \uc2 TOKYO/ZURICH June 14 (Reuters) - The threat of turmoil sweeping across global markets next week if Greece's election prompts a panicky flight of money from the euro zone has policymakers from Beijing to Zurich preparing to protect their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economies from an unwelcome influx.\par \par \uc2 Swiss National Bank President Thomas Jordan is among the most vociferous, dangling the threat on Thursday of imposing capital controls to stop the Swiss franc from soaring as a result of investors seeking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's relative safety.\par \par \uc2 "The SNB will not tolerate this," he said bluntly.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland is not alone. The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is prioritising market stability, according to one source, with economists saying the bank's main concern would be to stop the yen taking off.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a likely response should the yen rise too high for the authorities' taste. With G20 leaders meeting in Mexico next week there is even speculation of a coordinated global response although no evidence of that has emerged so far.\par \par \uc2 India has a range of crisis management groups within the government set up to deal with euro zone-triggered financial stress, according to Kaushik Basu, the finance minister's chief economic adviser.\par \par \uc2 In China, key agencies including the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have been asked to come up with similar plans, sources said last week. Measures may include keeping the yuan steady and stepping up policies to stabilise the economy, they said.\par \par \uc2 The big concern for all these countries - and others across Europe and the Americas - is that a victory on Sunday by parties in Greece opposed to austerity attached to its second bailout will send the euro zone further into crisis by pushing the country towards the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc's exit door.\par \par \uc2 There are already signs of contagion. Spanish 10-year bond yields rose above 7 percent for the first time in the euro era on Thursday, hitting a level widely seen as being unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 It has all triggered concerns about another global financial market spasm similar to the one that followed the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in 2008.\par \par \uc2 "Europe's debt problems are the biggest risk to the global and Japanese economies," BOJ Governor \uc2 Masaaki Shirakawa\uc2  told parliament this week. "A loss of market stability will lead to a severe economic slump, as we experienced during the Lehman crisis."\par \par \uc2 Norway could also suffer a hot money surge. It could cut interest rates in extremis to curb its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and has a monetary policy meeting next week, but with an already thriving economy it would risk overheating.\par \par \uc2 Fellow euro outsider Denmark is in a similar camp. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a top Swiss central banker, said last month that they were looking at the possibility of deploying negative interest rates.\par \par \uc2 DEFENDING THE FORT\par \par \uc2 Switzerland is already working to protect its economy from uncontrollable franc strength, a condition that damages exports and raises the danger of deflation.\par \par \uc2 It imposed a cap of 1.20 francs to the euro last September and pledged on Thursday to defend it.\par \par \uc2 "Even at the current rate, the Swiss franc is still high. Another appreciation would have a serious impact on both prices and the economy in Switzerland," Jordan, the SNB chairman, said.\par \par \uc2 "If necessary (the bank) stands ready to take further measures at any time."\par \par \uc2 Jordan did not say whether capital controls - stopping money from flowing in and out - were under consideration, but he pointedly did not rule them out, saying: "We are continuously looking at all possible other measures."\par \par \uc2 Sources in Tokyo said capital controls had been discounted in Japan because of the size of the economy, the world's third largest.\par \par \uc2 But with the shock of the 2008/2009 crisis still fresh, the country is not taking any chances. Indeed, many of the policies adopted in 2008/2009 are still in place, such as near zero \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates and easier collateral terms for short-term funding \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  still has a Lehman-legacy dollar-swap arrangement with the U.S. Federal Reserve, as do others. The latest extension of the deal, which allows the BOJ to tap unlimited amounts of dollars, runs to February 2013.\par \par \uc2 The most probable scenario Japanese policymakers are looking at is a flood of money rushing into yen assets, according to several interviews in the past week with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government officials who craft economic policy.\par \par \uc2 Money has flowed into the yen fairly persistently since the euro area crisis erupted in late 2009 and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a record high of 75.31 yen per dollar in October.\par \par \uc2 Further upward pressure would spark fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities, especially after the IMF said this week such a measure was an option to ease volatility. The BOJ could also ease policy by increasing the spending limit on its main tool - a 40 trillion yen ($504 billion) asset buying fund.\par \par \uc2 (Written by Jeremy Gaunt and Leika Kihara. Editing by Mike Peacock)\par \par \uc2 EUROZONE-CONTINGENCY/ | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | G | RBN | J | Z | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS | ABX | BNX | FUN | SNI | RNP | DNP | PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120614e86e000p2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
