\par\par \b \uc2 DJ 2nd UPDATE: IMF Says Japan Must Do More Vs Deflation, Offers Sympathy on Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2012\par 09:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --IMF says it now believes the yen is overvalued, and is sympathetic toward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --IMF encourages the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to expand its asset-purchase program "substantially" to achieve 1% inflation\par \par \uc2 --IMF recommends the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buy longer-dated JGBs, private-sector shares\par \par \uc2 --IMF believe Japan's banks are in good shape to withstand shocks, including a possible downturn in the bond market\par \par \uc2 (Combines material from an interview with IMF deputy managing director Lipton, comments on the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bank stress tests, 6th-17th paragraphs) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Eleanor Warnock \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO--The International Monetary Fund said Tuesday that the Japanese government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  must do more to combat deflation, but the multilateral lender also expressed sympathy for Tokyo's controversial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meant to limit the negative effects of a strong yen on the export-dependant economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a report following its annual policy meeting with Japanese officials, the IMF said the Bank of Japan is capable of further measures, such as the purchase of longer-term government bonds as well as private-sector debt and equity to meet its recently announced 1% inflation goal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fund also said "further easing of monetary policy would be advisable" if Japan's economic outlook worsens sharply amid the European debt crisis and slowing global growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is already engaged in what it calls "powerful" monetary easing, spearheaded by Y70 trillion ($875 billion) in asset purchases, largely government debt. But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is under strong political pressure to do more to weaken the yen and reverse entrenched deflation. At over 200% of annual GDP, the nation's huge public debt makes more government spending difficult.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Notwithstanding the current difficult environment for designing and implementing monetary policy, IMF staff believe that additional effective easing can be delivered," the fund said in the statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It also called on the government to put its fiscal house in order and undertake structural changes to the economy to promote growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Addressing Japan's fiscal vulnerabilities will require sustained adjustment and bold efforts to raise long-term growth," it said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But there was a bright side for policymakers, with the IMF offering a new, much more sympathetic view toward Japan's concerns about the negative effects to the economy from a strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It said the exchange rate has risen as global investors seek safe havens, "and our analysis suggests that the yen is moderately overvalued from a medium-term perspective."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of the delegation, First Deputy Managing Director David Lipton, offered even more solace, saying the IMF was sympathetic to the idea of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market--action that has been sharply criticized by some of Japan's trading partners.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If market forces lead to disruptive flows, short-term smoothing is something that we would understand," Mr. Lipton said in an interview with the Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The new position would seem to give some breathing room for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Since September 2010, it has made four forays into the market as the yen set a series of post-war highs against the dollar. While it has fallen somewhat from its late-October high, the yen has remained stubbornly strong as turmoil in Europe invites "safe-haven" buying despite near-zero Japanese interest rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A former U.S. Treasury Department official, Mr. Lipton reiterated the IMF's long-held \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  position that advanced economies should allow their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to trade freely and Japan shouldn't "guide the exchange rate to any particular level."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he indicated some exceptions can be made for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against excess volatility, saying "in the present market circumstances, we understand that there is a lot of volatility."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Lipton also offered good news for Japan's banks, saying the latest so-called stress tests show they are in good shape to withstand any foreseeable shocks. There have been concerns that the banks could suffer huge valuation losses on their extensive holdings of Japanese government bonds if demand falters and yields start to rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think we find that not only are the banks robust and have come through the crisis in the United States, the crisis in Europe very well, but that the banks are strong enough to withstand substantial shocks," adding that potential shocks include the scenario of a rise in the currently low domestic rates. He said Japan's big banks were in especially good condition.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At present the benchmark 10-year JGB is yielding just 0.85%. Japanese banks are the largest holders of JGBs at 41% of the total outstanding.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Takashi Nakamichi at takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 12, 2012 03:57 ET (07:57 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120612e86c001l5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
