\par\par \b \uc2 DJ BOJ WATCH: BOJ and Govt Cool to Lawmaker Calls to Buy Foreign Bonds\b0\par\par\uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2012\par 06:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Some lawmakers want BOJ to buy foreign bonds to weaken yen and push up prices\par \par \uc2 -- BOJ and government cool to idea as it will be seen as another form of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Tatsuo Ito and Eleanor Warnock\par \par \uc2 TOKYO--Faced with the yen's persistent strength, some lawmakers are calling on the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  to start buying foreign bonds to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, but both the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the government are cool to the idea of resorting to such an unconventional policy.\par \par \uc2 The yen was approaching a historical high this week before it reversed course after a teleconference among the Group of Seven finance ministers, reviving discussions about the BOJ buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated bonds.\par \par \uc2 Those in favor of the idea argue that it is more effective tool to end deflation than the BOJ's existing policies.\par \par \uc2 "Buying (foreign bonds) will even have an effect on the exchange rate by weakening the yen and thus have a positive effect on the economy," Kozo Yamamoto, a lawmaker with the largest opposition Liberal Democratic Party, said recently.\par \par \uc2 Buying foreign assets would be accompanied by selling of the yen, which would work to push down its value. The strong yen has added to deflationary pressure in Japan as a fall in corporate profits overseas forces them to cut costs at home to maintain competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 A group of LDP lawmakers including Yamamoto, have come up with a proposal to revise the law governing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow it to buy foreign assets, a notion that Yamamoto concedes could be perceived as "controversial."\par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  passively holds around Y4.3 trillion in overseas assets, it is still prohibited from buying foreign assets with the aim of affecting the yen's exchange rate. The overseas assets are accumulated interest payments from foreign assets that it received from the finance ministry decades ago.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the idea of BOJ buying foreign bonds, BOJ Gov. \uc2 Masaaki Shirakawa\uc2  said last month that it was up to the government to decide, noting that the exchange rate policy was in the hands of the finance minister.\par \par \uc2 When \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the finance minister hands down the decision, and the BOJ conducts the transaction, but only as an agent.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Finance Minister Jun Azumi struck a cautious note about the BOJ purchases. "We must be deal with it cautiously," Azumi told a news conference.\par \par \uc2 Government officials say they remain opposed to the BOJ buying foreign bonds with yen, as it could be construed as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and could invite backlash from the U.S. and other countries.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell the yen on several instances last year, most recently in November, but the action has come under criticism from the U.S. and European authorities.\par \par \uc2 One government official said even if the BOJ sold yen on its own, instead of the finance ministry, it would likely face overseas disapproval.\par \par \uc2 "Would it be accepted in the international community? I don't think so," the official said, adding that it will only be regarded as Japan pushing down the yen "through a different form."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold a regular meeting of its policy board on June 14 and 15.\par \par \uc2 Write to Tatsuo Ito +813-6269-2780; tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020120612e86b0030t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
