\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Citi says Watch for Japan if Dollar Drops to Y76-Handle\b0\par\par\uc2 1,288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2012\par 16:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 10:32 EDT - With Japanese jawboning spooking enough traders away from the yen, "if we stay at Y78-Y79, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely," says Citi's Greg Anderson. But if the dollar falls to a level with a Y76-handle, "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... fairly quickly," he says. Otherwise, Japanese officials are likely to stand back to see if there's a concerted effort by G7 finance and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials act in concert to prop their economies. Investors are trying to position for USD/JPY upside, but without taking a lot of risk, Anderson says. If there is dip in USD/JPY to a Y76 handle, investors would buy dollars, but otherwise they are standing aside. USD/JPY recently at 78.68, up 0.42%, according to EBS via CQG. (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:27 EDT - Demand for gold will increase as the world's largest consumers of the metal--China and India--cut interest rates to stimulate growth, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  predicts. It foresees China enacting a 1/4-point rate cut and lowering the reserve-requirement ratio to 18% from 20%. In India, falling oil prices could spur such moves as inflation fears might ease a bit. "More accommodative monetary policies...may have positive implications for gold prices," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says, as investors buy gold to hedge against potential inflation. (nicole.friedman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:24 EDT - \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  is confident in uptake of \uc2 Edwards Lifesciences\uc2 ' (EW) catheter-delivered replacement heart valve ahead of June 13 FDA panel. May's decision on Medicare payment for procedure "should provide hospitals and physicians with greater clarity and certainty around reimbursement," firm writes in a note. Wells also expects company to have a positive FDA panel next week as it seeks to gain approval of device for high-risk patients with severe narrowing of the aortic valve. EW currently has approval in US for inoperable patients and is trying to expand market for the device. EW up 1.1% to $85.07. (anjali.athavaley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:22 EDT - \uc2 Navistar\uc2  (NAV) opened at a 3-year low at $25.50 before rebounding with the broader market as Baird downgrades the truck and engine maker to neutral and slashes its price target to $31 from $56. It has been consistently bullish on NAV, but the investment bank--and others on Wall Street--are anticipating a dismal F2Q report Thursday. Baird says NAV's ongoing struggles to comply with the EPA's latest engine-emissions regulations are keeping truck buyers on the sidelines and the company's margins under pressure. NAV is paying EPA fines for selling noncompliant engines. Meanwhile, Baird notes NAV's share of the North American heavy-duty truck market slipped to about 17% in the quarter from 20%-plus previously. The stock is now up 0.4% at $26.72. (robert.tita@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 10:14 EDT - Refuting the trend of downbeat reports, the ISM non-manufacturing report shows service sector picking up, with PMI edging up to 53.7 in May, from 53.5 in April. But employment index, at 50.8, is barely in expansion mode, suggesting service companies are increasing production without adding many workers. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:09 EDT Financial stocks in the S&P 500 getting a break from their role as stock-market whipping boy. The sector is up 1.6% as the S&P 500 adds about five points early on. For the DJIA, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  (BAC) and \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  (JPM) among the biggest percentage gainers. S&P 500 financials the worst-performing sector during the past month. (matthew.jarzemsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:07 EDT - ISM non-manufacturing index came in a tad above expectations, allowing investors to stay out of safe-haven Treasurys. The market has erased all of Friday's post-payrolls gains now, with benchmark 10-year notes down 10/32 to yield 1.563%. The 30-year bond continues to lead the decline, down 1 3/32 to yield 2.621%. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:03 EDT - Canaccord Genuity favors big-cap over smaller-cap engineering and construction stocks, noting the respective groups' valuations trade in line on a forward EBITDA basis and history has shown when this happens large cap outperform their small-cap brethren 75% of the time. Among big-cap names, firm favors \uc2 SNC-Lavalin\uc2  (SNC.T), \uc2 Fluor\uc2  (FLR), and \uc2 Foster Wheeler\uc2  (FWLT). Bolstered by healthy cash positions and big order backlogs, the stocks should outperform as investor risk appetite increases. And in the event economic conditions worsen, their balance sheets and backlogs will help them weather the difficult times, Canaccord Genuity says. (ben.dummett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:02 EDT - Government of Canada bonds edged slightly higher after \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  reaffirmed its modest and conditional tightening bias in its policy statement, but then relinquished much of that advance. Two-years remain underwater, trimming declines immediately after BoC statement but then ceding much of that gain, and 10-yrs mimic that pattern. Two-years yielding 1.003%, up from 1.001% just before and from 0.975% late Mon, 10-years at 1.707%, unchanged from their pre-statement level but up from 1.055% late Monday. Fluctuations in global risk sentiment in response to problems in the euro zone will likely continue to trump domestic issues as drivers of the Canadian market. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:56 EDT - Apparently, five heads are better than one. \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  (KO) joins with Ford (F), \uc2 Heinz (HNZ)\uc2 , \uc2 Nike\uc2  (NKE) and \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  (PG) to work on speeding up the development of plant-based plastics. KO has been at the forefront of the development with its PlantBottle, which is partially made from plant-based material and is used by its Dasani water brand. HNZ also licenses the product for its ketchup bottles, but now it looks like more companies are getting into the game. The product scores environmental points, and also helps companies reduce their reliance on fossil fuels. (paul.ziobro@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:55 EDT - As Nomura projects 30% odds of the ECB cutting rates Wednesday, "the higher likelihood" is one occurring next month. It adds chances of another LTRO are low, too. Market participants have pointed out that liquidity created because of the previous efforts is keeping funding stress low. The 3-month euro-dollar cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basis swap is at -55.5 basis points, virtually unchanged from Monday, even though it had widened out briefly to -59 late in the day. "The price is jumpy because UK is still on holiday," says Nomura. "Less liquidity makes the change bigger." (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:48 EDT - Akamai (AKAM) leading decliners on the S&P 500 after Netflix (NFLX) reveals it is building its own content delivery network to fire off video data. Jefferies has its doubts, saying credibility issues surround the project as "this is not NFLX's first effort" at bringing content delivery in house. Still, the firm sees the migration of traffic away from commercial vendors as a negative development, adding near-term blowback will likely fall to AKAM peer \uc2 Limelight Networks\uc2  (LLNW) given its "sizable" NFLX exposure. AKAM last off 6.8% to $26.39 while LLNW trading 10% lower at $2.42. (mia.lamar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 06-05-12 1032ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120605e8650009j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
