\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Asian Markets Stumble\b0\par\par\uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juni 2012\par 15:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Daniel Inman \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong joined indexes from Australia to Japan in wiping out their gains for the year Monday, as fallout from Europe's debt crisis and fresh signs of a slowdown in the U.S. and China drove markets lower.\par \par \uc2 Since the first quarter, when Asia joined in the rapid global stock rally, markets in the region have fixated on worries that Greece may leave the euro zone and the health of Spain's banking system.\par \par \uc2 Growing evidence of a deceleration in China is forcing investors to concentrate on regional issues too.\par \par \uc2 "The data is turning. Hard," Frederic Neumann, co-head of Asian economics at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said in a note to clients. "The trade cycle is about to take a hit. Globally, new export orders have started to contract for the first time since December."\par \par \uc2 The flight to safety is boosting the allure of sovereign bonds in Asia. The yield on the 10-year Japanese government bond dropped to as low as 0.79%, the lowest since June 2003.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen has been bought heavily as investors shun risky assets. On Monday, though, the yen halted its advance, with the dollar trading at 78.03 yen on speculation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker to aid exporters.\par \par \uc2 "We have gone from capitulation to revulsion," said Sean Darby, chief global equity strategist at Jefferies in Hong Kong. "People at the bottom of the cycle don't want to have anything to do with equities, so you have these selloffs."\par \par \uc2 Leading the declines was Taiwan's Taiex, down 3%, while the China Shanghai Composite Index fell 2.7%. Japan's Topix index, a broader measure of Japanese equities than the narrower Nikkei 225, slumped 1.9% to its lowest level since 1983.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei ended the day 1.7% down, the Hang Seng Index 2% down, and Australia's S&P ASX 200 fell 1.9%. The Nikkei has now dropped 19% from its March peak, while the Hang Seng Index is down 16% from its February high. The early-year advance followed by a fade before the end of the first half is similar to the pattern of the past two years.\par \par \uc2 The combination of weaker exports and lower risk tolerance by investors in Asia is expected to further slow economies over the coming months. On the positive side, inflationary pressures, which had been the key worry among policy makers for most of this year, have mostly abated, opening the door for more significant stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "There is a separate Asian theme, emanating from China, that is partly behind the re-pricing," said Tim Condon, economist at ING.\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, exports unexpectedly fell for a third straight month in May, as manufacturing activity there showed signs of weakness. In India, the government reported last week that economic growth fell to its lowest level in nine years.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Asian fund managers shifted weighting of global equities to "slightly underweight" from April's "neutral," as concerns over Europe have grown. It was the first move below the "neutral" footing since December.\par \par \uc2 Gold is returning to its haven status, rising 3.3% last week. It was little changed Monday at $1,618.50 an ounce, as economic reports in China and U.S. point to a deteriorating picture.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the U.S. reported nonfarm payrolls rose 69,000 in May, the smallest increase in a year, while unemployment in the world's largest economy edged up to 8.2%, the first increase in just under a year. Earlier on Friday, two reports showed that activity in the Chinese manufacturing sector is slowing as officials signal there are no plans to reinstate an economic stimulus on the magnitude of the four-trillion-yuan ($628 billion) package in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Around the world, potential market land mines dot the horizon. Greece holds elections on June 17 that will largely determine its course. The U.S. Federal Reserve's rate-setting committee, scheduled for June 19 and 20, could signal leanings toward further stimulus. And June 28 and 29, a summit of European Union leaders in Brussels will be closely watched by investors for evidence that policy makers will increase efforts to solve the region's crisis.\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been reluctant to step into the crisis, insisting that governments take the lead. But political leaders have been unable to agree on solutions.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Write to Daniel Inman  at daniel.inman@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 06-04-12 0930ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120604e8640006m\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\cl